window.CARDS.push({
id:'32', n:32, w:1,
q:'What are the principles of metabolic regulation?',
qcn:'代谢调控的基本原则',
sub:'主干说完约 90 秒 · 三个层级 ＋ 一条「控在哪一步」的规矩 · 例子全部挂在别的卡上',

/* ---------------- 速背 ---------------- */
cram:[
 {g:'开口', gn:'题面三个实词都要交代：principles 是什么意思、metabolic 指什么、regulation 指什么。<b>最要紧的是先声明这是「规则」不是「清单」</b>——你一开口就把答案的形状定下来了，他就不会追着你要通路名。'},
 {n:'01', t:'定义 · 三个词各一句',
  ez:'“Metabolism is the whole set of enzyme reactions in a living cell. They are strictly coordinated. The textbook divides it in two. Catabolism is the degrading half. It takes nutrients apart. And it releases energy. Anabolism is the synthetic half. It builds the cell’s own molecules. And it spends energy. Regulation means setting the rate of those reactions. So the cell makes what it needs. And it does not make what it does not need. By principles I mean the general rules. They are rules about where control sits and how it acts. I do not mean a list of pathways.”',
  ezcn:'<b>代谢 ＝ 活细胞里全部严格协调的酶反应。</b><b>书把它分成两半</b>：<b>分解代谢是拆的那一半——把养分拆开、放出能量</b>；<b>合成代谢是造的那一半——造细胞自己的分子、花掉能量</b>。<b>调控 ＝ 设定这些反应的速率</b>，<b>好让细胞造它需要的，不造它不需要的</b>。<b>而「原理」指的是「控制点在哪儿、以什么方式起作用」的通则，不是一张通路清单。</b>',
  big:'<b>metabolism</b> ＝ 细胞里那一整套严格协同的酶反应（分解 ＋ 合成）· <b>regulation</b> ＝ 设定这些反应的速率 · <b>principles</b> ＝ 控在哪、怎么控的<b>通则</b>，不是通路清单',
  en:'“Metabolism is the whole set of strictly coordinated enzyme reactions that run in a living cell. The textbook divides it in two. <b>Catabolism</b> is the degrading half. It takes nutrients apart and releases energy. <b>Anabolism</b> is the synthetic half. It builds the cell’s own molecules and spends energy. <b>Regulation</b> means setting the rate of those reactions. So the cell makes what it needs, and not what it does not need. By <b>principles</b> I mean the general rules about <b>where</b> control sits and <b>how</b> it acts. I do not mean a list of pathways.”',
  note:'<b>「不是通路清单」这半句是这题的分水岭。</b>不说它，你就会滑进「糖酵解怎么调、循环怎么调、脂肪酸怎么调」，讲三分钟还没答到 principles，而且每报一条通路就是递给他一把刀。说了它，后面所有例子都变成<b>规则的证据</b>，而不是被考的内容。<br>书 p218 开篇的定义原话（英译）：<i>Intermediary metabolism means the sum of strictly mutually coordinated reactions that run in a living cell</i>（中间代谢 ＝ 活细胞里一整套互相严格协同的反应）；<i>The degrading part … is called catabolism, and the sum of the synthetic reactions forms anabolism</i>。<span class="pg">p218</span>'},
 {n:'02', t:'为什么非调不可（书自己的开场白）',
  ez:'“The textbook opens the chapter with the reason. Suppose the reactions simply coexisted, uncontrolled. Then enzyme reactions in many places would run uselessly. And the effects of some enzyme systems would cancel each other out. A high degree of organisation is possible only one way. It needs consistent regulation of the enzyme reactions.”',
  ezcn:'<b>书用这个理由开的章：假如这些反应只是不受控地共存，代谢里很多地方的酶反应就会白跑，而某些酶系统的作用会互相抵消。</b><b>高度的组织化只有一个前提：对这些酶反应进行一贯的调控。</b>',
  big:'不控制的话，酶反应会在很多地方<b>白跑</b>，有些酶系统还会<b>互相抵消</b>',
  en:'“The textbook opens the chapter with the reason. Under uncontrolled coexistence, enzyme reactions in many places of metabolism would run uselessly, and the effects of some enzyme systems would cancel each other out. A high degree of organisation is possible only under consistent regulation of the enzyme reactions.”',
  note:'<b>这是书 §10.2 的第一句，几乎可以照搬。</b>原文（英译）：<i>Under uncontrolled coexistence, enzyme reactions in many places of metabolism would run uselessly, and the effects of some enzyme systems would cancel each other out. A high degree of organisation is possible only under consistent regulation of the enzyme reactions.</i><br><b>「互相抵消」这四个字值得单独强调</b>——它就是后面「两条相反通路要互控」那一格的种子（点 10）。同时合成又分解，等于把 ATP 烧成热。<span class="pg">p221</span>'},
 {n:'03', t:'调控有几个尺度',
  ez:'“The textbook says regulation can be considered at different levels. The metabolism of a whole organism is one level. Then an organ. Then a cell. Then a system of enzyme reactions. Then a single enzyme reaction. Physiologically it classifies regulation as nervous or hormonal. Then it narrows down. It narrows to the single enzyme reaction. And to systems of enzyme reactions. And so will I.”',
  ezcn:'<b>书先说调控可以在不同层次上考察</b>：<b>整个机体的代谢 → 一个器官 → 一个细胞 → 一个酶反应系统 → 单个酶反应</b>。<b>生理上它把调控分成神经的和激素的</b>；<b>然后它把范围收到「单个酶反应」和「酶反应系统」上——我也这么收。</b>',
  big:'整个机体 → 器官 → 细胞 → <b>一套酶反应</b> → <b>单个酶反应</b>；生理上分<b>神经</b>和<b>激素</b>两类。书只细讲最后两级',
  en:'“The textbook says first that regulation can be considered at different levels. The metabolism of a whole organism, of an organ, of a cell, of a system of enzyme reactions, and of a single enzyme reaction. Physiologically it classifies regulation as <b>nervous</b> or <b>hormonal</b>. Then it narrows to the single enzyme reaction and to systems of enzyme reactions, and so will I.”',
  note:'<b>先报尺度、再说自己收在哪一级，是研究者的答法</b>——你在告诉他你知道这题可以答得多大，而你是<b>选择</b>了一个范围，不是只知道这么多。<br>书原话（英译）：<i>Regulation of metabolic processes can be considered at various levels … We concentrate on the possibilities of regulating a single enzyme reaction and systems of enzyme reactions.</i><br>⚠ <b>「神经调控」全书只有这一句</b>，不要往下接。他要是追，说这本书在这一章只把它作为分类列出，细节在生理学那边。<span class="pg">p221</span>'},

 {g:'三个层级', gn:'把答案按<b>「控的是什么」</b>排成三条：<b>有多少酶 · 现有的酶开不开 · 酶和底物碰不碰得到</b>。这三条是通用的，任何通路都套得进去，所以它们才是 principles。'},
 {n:'04', t:'层级一 · 酶的<b>数量</b>',
  big:'控<b>合成多少酶</b>：<b>induction（诱导）</b> 和 <b>repression（阻遏）</b>。书说这一级动用的是细胞的<b>遗传装备</b>。慢，改的是<b>上限</b>',
  en:'“The first level is the <b>amount</b> of enzyme. The cell controls how much of an enzyme exists, by controlling its synthesis. The textbook calls this regulation of enzyme biosynthesis by <b>induction</b> or <b>repression</b>, and it says this is the level at which the cell uses its genetic equipment. An inducer can start or raise the transcription and then the translation of that protein. Transcription and translation can be suppressed the same way. The textbook adds that <b>steroid hormones</b> induce enzyme biosynthesis. The worked examples are the <b>lac and trp operons</b>. This level is slow, because a protein has to be made, and it changes the ceiling rather than the rate from second to second.”',
  note:'<b>「改上限，不改此刻的速度」这一句是三个层级能被分开的理由</b>，也是他最可能追的一刀（「那诱导和变构有什么区别」）。诱导要转录 ＋ 翻译，是分钟到小时；变构是毫秒。<br>书 §10.2 这一段写的是 <i>regulation of enzyme biosynthesis by induction or repression (section 4.2.4)</i>，<b>并明说 <i>this is the level at which the cell’s genetic equipment is used</i></b>（这是使用细胞遗传装备的层级）。操纵子机制在 <span class="pg">p102 p103 p104</span>，<b>整套在卡 12</b>——考场上说一句「the lac and trp operons」就够，他要展开你再接。<span class="pg">p221</span>'},
 {n:'05', t:'层级二 · 现有酶的<b>活性</b>（三条分开说）',
  big:'① <b>变构</b>（非共价、可逆、毫秒）② <b>共价修饰</b>（另一个酶来改，主要是磷酸化）③ <b>抑制剂结合进活性部位</b>（产物自己就能干）',
  en:'“The second level is the <b>activity</b> of enzyme molecules that already exist. This is fast, because nothing has to be made. The textbook gives three ways. First, <b>allosteric control</b>. A metabolite binds at a site other than the active site. The binding changes the conformation of the enzyme, and the conformation change is what changes the activity. The textbook calls allosteric activation and inhibition one of the most effective mechanisms of metabolic regulation. Second, <b>covalent modification</b>. Another enzyme changes this enzyme chemically, most often by phosphorylation. Third, <b>binding of an inhibitor</b> into the active site. The product of the reaction can do this itself, so the reaction slows down while its product piles up.”',
  note:'<b>三条要分开报，不要合并成「酶活性受调节」。</b>它们在三个判据上都不同，而这三个判据正是他会追的：<br>① <b>共价还是非共价</b>：变构是非共价、效应物随时可以离开；磷酸化是共价、必须另一个酶来拆。<br>② <b>要不要花 ATP</b>：变构不花；磷酸化每装一次花一个 ATP 上的磷酸基。<br>③ <b>可不可逆</b>：磷酸化可逆（有专门的磷酸酶）；<b>酶原被部分蛋白水解激活是不可逆的</b>（trypsinogen → trypsin），书 p064 明说这一类 <i>is this time irreversible</i>。<br>书 §10.2 的三个小标题（英译）分别是 <i>Inhibition of enzyme reactions</i>、<i>Allosteric control</i>、<i>Regulation by enzyme modification</i>；变构的定义在 <span class="pg">p062</span>，共价修饰在 <span class="pg">p063 p064</span>。<span class="pg">p221 p222</span>'},
 {n:'06', t:'层级三 · 酶和底物<b>碰不碰得到</b>',
  big:'<b>compartmentation（区室化）</b>：把反应关进不同的隔间 ＋ <b>limiting metabolite（限制性代谢物）</b>：把供应掐住。书的例子是<b>呼吸链磷酸化受 ADP 供应限制</b>',
  en:'“The third level is <b>access</b>. The cell controls whether the enzyme and the substrate ever meet. <b>Compartmentation</b> is the textbook’s word for the first half. The cell is divided into many spaces called compartments. Substrates and products have to be transported across the membranes that separate them, and the textbook says this transport is usually controlled. That is how synthesis and degradation can run separately even when they share some of their intermediates. The second half is <b>supply</b>. The textbook calls it regulation by limiting metabolites. The availability of a key metabolite sets how much product an enzyme or a whole system can make. Its example is phosphorylation in the respiratory chain. There the limiting factor is having enough ADP.”',
  note:'<b>compartmentation 这个词全套卡里只有这张有，所以要就地交代掉</b>（「细胞被分成许多隔间，底物和产物必须被运过隔开它们的膜」）。<br><b>ADP 那个例子非常好用</b>，因为它把调控接回了 #25 #26：呼吸链不是「有 NADH 就跑」，是「有 ADP 才跑」。<b>细胞不缺燃料的时候缺的是空电池。</b>书原话（英译）：<i>An example can be phosphorylation in the respiratory chain, where the limiting factor is a sufficient supply of ADP.</i><br>区室的实例书在别处给了：<b>脂肪酸合成在胞质的多酶复合体上，降解在线粒体里</b>（<span class="pg">p220</span>），<b>而且合成用 NADPH、氧化产 NADH</b>——<b>两种货币分开，就是最彻底的区室化</b>（见点 11）。<span class="pg">p222</span>'},

 {g:'控在哪一步', gn:'三个层级回答「用什么手段」，这一组回答<b>「装在哪个位置」</b>。<b>这一组是这题真正的分数所在</b>——手段谁都背得出，位置的判据背不出。'},
 {n:'07', t:'控在<b>实际上不可逆</b>的那一步',
  big:'书的原话：<b>controlling enzyme</b>（书 p222 印在那一节上的名字，＝控制酶）不一定靠被变构控制来起控制作用，也可以靠<b>催化一个实际上不可逆的反应</b>（例如强放能的反应）。例子：<b>phosphofructokinase</b>',
  en:'“Now the rule about <b>where</b>. The textbook says a controlling enzyme does not have to do its controlling job only by being allosterically controlled. It can also do it by catalysing a <b>practically irreversible</b> reaction, for example a strongly <b>exergonic</b> one. Exergonic means the reaction releases Gibbs energy, so it runs on its own and it does not run back. The textbook’s example is <b>phosphofructokinase</b> in glycolysis. It phosphorylates fructose-6-phosphate to fructose-1,6-bisphosphate. That phosphorylation is practically irreversible, and the textbook says this is what makes it the rate-limiting reaction of the whole glycolytic process. It is also the <b>committed step</b>, the first step after which the intermediate has nowhere else to go.”',
  note:'<b>「committed step」这个词书里没有，但它的内容书全给了，所以要自己下定义再用</b>（「过了这一步，中间产物就没有别的去处了」）。<br><b>为什么是 PFK 而不是前面的 hexokinase</b>，卡 gly 有现成答案：glucose-6-phosphate <b>还没有 committed</b>——它可以去糖原，也可以走戊糖磷酸途径（书 <span class="pg">p219</span> 两条出口都列了）。<b>fructose-1,6-bisphosphate 没有第二条出路，所以定局点在 PFK。</b>英文：“Glucose-6-phosphate is not yet committed. It can go into glycogen or into the pentose phosphate pathway. Fructose-1,6-bisphosphate has no other exit, so the commitment happens at phosphofructokinase.”<br><b>而且书顺手给了第二句</b>：<i>The activity of phosphofructokinase is allosterically controlled by the ATP/ADP system</i>——<b>同一个酶身上，位置的规矩和手段的规矩叠在一起</b>：它坐在不可逆步上（位置），又被 ATP/ADP 变构控制（手段）。这一句是这张卡最值钱的一句。<span class="pg">p164 p222</span>'},
 {n:'08', t:'为什么<b>不</b>控在接近平衡的那一步',
  big:'平衡的位置由热力学定死，<b>酶只改变到达平衡的快慢，不改变平衡在哪</b>。书把三个平衡组成印出来了，正好当证据',
  en:'“And here is the other half of the rule. Control at a step that sits near equilibrium would achieve nothing. Such an enzyme only speeds up the approach to an equilibrium whose position is already fixed. The textbook shows this without saying it. At the isomerase step it prints the equilibrium mixture as thirty per cent fructose-6-phosphate. At the aldolase step it prints eighty-nine per cent hexose against eleven per cent trioses. At the triose phosphate isomerase step it prints ninety-six per cent dihydroxyacetone phosphate. Those three numbers are properties of the reaction and not of the enzyme. So the flux is set where the reaction cannot come back, and that is the irreversible step.”',
  note:'<b>这是「用书的一半打书的另一半」的标准动作</b>：书从来没写过「不要控在平衡步」，但它在同一页把三个平衡组成印出来了，又在下一章说控制酶催化的是不可逆反应。<b>两处一拼，结论自己就出来了。</b>引用页码比引用道理稳。<span class="pg">p164 p222</span><br>⚠ <b>别把这条说成「改变可逆步的酶量完全没有影响」</b>——那是我加的、书没说的绝对化。<b>能说的是：平衡的位置不动。</b>他要是追，就说这一句，别往「控制系数」那边走（那是代谢控制分析，这本书没有）。<br><br><b>=== 公式和推导（背之前先看懂这一段） ===</b><br><b>式子</b>：<code>ΔG°′（总）= Σ ΔG°′（各步）</code>；<code>ΔG°′ &lt; 0 放能（exergonic，自己会走）</code>，<code>ΔG°′ &gt; 0 吸能（endergonic，自己不会走）</code><br>ΔG°′ 是标准 Gibbs 能变 · 单位 kJ/mol · 书 p131 明写「一串反应的总 Gibbs 能变等于各步之和」。<br><br><b>三步推导</b>（他问「凭什么说那一步不可逆」就照这个说，三个数全是书 p131 印的）：<br>① 单独的磷酸化<b>不会自己走</b>：<code>glucose + Pᵢ → glucose-6-phosphate + H₂O，ΔG°′ = +13,8</code>。<br>② ATP 水解<b>会自己走</b>：<code>ATP + H₂O → ADP + Pᵢ，ΔG°′ = −30,5</code>。<br>③ 偶联起来（hexokinase 那一步）：<code>glucose + ATP → glucose-6-phosphate + ADP，ΔG°′ = +13,8 − 30,5 = −16,7</code>。书直接把 −16,7 印在表里。<br><br><br><b>=== 一句话理解 ===</b><br><b>那 30,5 里只有 13,8 变成了糖上的那个磷酸键，剩下的 16,7 是故意丢掉的</b>——它不做功，它买的是<b>「这一步不会往回走」</b>。<br>所以<b>不可逆不是一个化学性质，是一笔付过钱的性质</b>。细胞肯在某一步付这笔钱，就是因为它想在那一步装阀门。<br><b>把这一句说出来，「为什么控制点都在不可逆步上」就不用再解释了</b>：阀门只能装在已经付过钱的地方。<br>⚠ 书印的是 ΔG°′（标准态），不是细胞里的实际 ΔG；<b>「practically irreversible」这个说法是书自己用的词</b>（书 p164 p222 印的就是这个说法），照书说最稳。'},
 {n:'09', t:'反馈抑制 —— 最经典的那一条',
  big:'<b>终产物回头抑制这条路的第一个酶</b>，那个酶通常是变构酶。书叫它<b>负反馈</b>，叫那个酶 <b>controlling enzyme</b>（书印的名字，＝控制酶）',
  en:'“The classic pattern is <b>feedback inhibition</b>. The textbook writes a chain. A is converted through the intermediates B, C, D and E into the product F, and each step has its own enzyme. Only F acts on the first enzyme of the whole sequence, and it inhibits it. That first enzyme is an allosteric enzyme, and the textbook calls it the <b>controlling enzyme</b>. The textbook says this is the general mechanism for regulating biosynthetic processes, worked out above all in bacteria, and that it minimises the demand for carbon, nitrogen and energy. Its worked example is the synthesis of isoleucine from threonine through five intermediates. The first enzyme of that sequence dehydrates threonine, and isoleucine itself inhibits it.”',
  note:'<b>「为什么是第一个酶，不是最后一个」是他几乎一定会追的一刀。</b>答案在书自己给的理由里：<i>In this way the demands for carbon, nitrogen and energy are minimised</i>。英文：“Because inhibiting the last enzyme would stop the product but leave all the intermediates already built. Inhibiting the first enzyme means nothing is spent at all. The textbook says this minimises the demand for carbon, nitrogen and energy.”<br><b>第二个实例书也给了</b>：嘧啶核苷酸的合成被控在 N-carbamoylaspartate 生成那一步，<b>aspartate transcarbamoylase</b> 是变构酶，被整条路的终产物 <b>CTP</b> 抑制。<span class="pg">p072</span> 英文：“The textbook’s other case is pyrimidine nucleotide synthesis. Aspartate transcarbamoylase catalyses the first reaction and is allosteric, and CTP, the end product of the whole process, inhibits it.”<br><br><b>画的顺序（他可能让你画，也可能让你认）</b>：<code>A —E₁→ B —E₂→ C —E₃→ D —E₄→ E —E₅→ F</code>，然后<b>从 F 画一条虚线弯回 E₁</b>，在箭头端画一个横杠表示抑制，最后在 E₁ 底下写 <code>allosteric</code>。书 <span class="pg">p062</span> 的那张小图就是这个，三笔画完。'},
 {n:'10', t:'一个酶 vs <b>两条相反的通路</b>',
  big:'糖原：<b>合成酶活的时候磷酸化酶就是失活的，反过来也一样</b>。一个 cAMP、一个蛋白激酶，<b>同时把一边打开、把另一边关上</b>',
  en:'“There is a difference between regulating one enzyme and regulating two opposing pathways together. The textbook’s case is glycogen. It says synthesis and breakdown of glycogen are coordinately controlled by an amplifying cascade of reactions. Glycogen synthase is active when phosphorylase is inactive, and the other way round. Adrenaline and glucagon stimulate breakdown and inhibit synthesis by raising the level of <b>cyclic AMP</b> inside the cell, and cyclic AMP activates a <b>protein kinase</b>. The same kinase phosphorylates both enzymes. Phosphorylation inactivates glycogen synthase, and phosphorylation converts phosphorylase into its much more active form. <b>One signal, one enzyme, two opposite answers.</b> That is what reciprocal regulation means, and it is how the cell avoids building and destroying the same molecule at the same time.”',
  note:'<b>这一格直接回答了点 02 里「有些酶系统会互相抵消」那句警告</b>——书在第 10 章开头提出的问题，在第 7 章用糖原解决了。<b>把这两处接起来说，是这张卡最漂亮的一个动作。</b><br>书 <span class="pg">p174</span> 原话（英译）：<i>Synthesis and degradation of glycogen are coordinately controlled by an amplifying cascade of reactions. Glycogen synthase is active when the phosphorylase is inactive, and the other way round.</i> 级联细节在 <span class="pg">p175</span>：cAMP → protein kinase → 一边磷酸化 phosphorylase kinase（于是 phosphorylase b → a，活化），一边磷酸化 glycogen synthase（失活）；<b>拆磷酸的是专门的 protein phosphatases</b>。<br>⚠ <b>「同一个激酶两个底物」这一点必须说准</b>：书说 <i>the protein kinase, the same enzyme that inactivates glycogen synthase by phosphorylation</i>——就是同一个酶。这正是「reciprocal」二字的机制根据，不是修辞。<br><b>糖原本身的结构和 phosphorylase 的化学在卡 06 和卡 gly。</b>'},
 {n:'11', t:'前提：分解和合成<b>不是同一条路倒着走</b>',
  big:'书 p218：两者<b>走不同的路、用不同的酶、在不同的区室</b>，<b>正因为分开，才可以被独立调控</b>',
  en:'“One principle sits underneath all the others. The textbook says that catabolic and anabolic routes between the same precursor and the same end product are <b>not the reverse of one another</b>. They run by different routes. The reason it gives is the fundamental difference in their energy demands. Catabolic processes produce ATP, and anabolic reactions require energy and consume ATP during their course. The textbook then draws the conclusion itself. Because the routes are separate, they can be regulated independently. It adds that they are often placed in different parts of the eukaryotic cell, and that this separation lets both run at the same time and still independently.”',
  note:'<b>这一条是「可以调控」的前提，不是一个例子。</b>没有它，前面三个层级都没有落脚点：同一条路正反两个方向共用同一套酶，你就没有办法只关一个方向。<br>书 <span class="pg">p218</span> 原话（英译）：<i>Catabolic and anabolic processes between a precursor and a final product are not, however, the reverse of one another; they run by different routes … The fact that these routes are separate has the advantage that they can be regulated independently.</i><br>书 <span class="pg">p134</span> §6.1.3 又说了一遍，而且更狠：<b>把代谢拆成很多步本身就是为了可调</b>——<i>such a subdivided metabolism is regulatable (section 10.2)</i>，而独立调控的另一个可能<i>在于这些过程有不同的酶装备，并且定位在细胞的不同区室里</i>。<br><b>三个现成的实例，全在书里，报一个就够</b>：<br>① <b>脂肪酸</b>：合成在胞质的多酶复合体上、还原力用 <b>NADPH</b>；降解在线粒体里、氧化产 <b>NADH</b>（<span class="pg">p220</span>）。<br>② <b>糖</b>：糖异生不是糖酵解倒放——丙酮酸变回 PEP 要<b>绕道羧化成 oxaloacetate</b>，两次己糖磷酸化不可逆，合成时得换别的酶（<span class="pg">p219</span>）。<br>③ <b>戊糖磷酸途径</b>产的 NADPH <b>不进呼吸链</b>，专供合成反应（<span class="pg">p219</span>）。<br>英文（他追「举个例」时用）：“In fatty acid metabolism the hydrogen donor for synthesis is NADPH, while oxidation produces NADH. Synthesis runs on a multienzyme complex in the cytoplasm, and degradation is localised in the mitochondria. The textbook also says the NADPH from the pentose phosphate pathway is not reoxidised in the respiratory chain but used for synthetic reactions.”'},

 {g:'账', gn:'代谢题的通用要求：<b>投入 / 产出 / 净账</b>三行。这题的账不是 ATP 的账，是<b>调控本身的账</b>——调控要花钱，花的是 Gibbs 能量，买的是控制权。<b>只说「调控有什么好处」是半张账，他一句「那代价呢」就能把你问停。</b>'},
 {n:'12', t:'投入（调控要花什么）',
  big:'① 造调节蛋白要转录 ＋ 翻译 ② 每开关一次共价修饰花掉<b>一个 ATP</b> ③ <b>不可逆那一步的 Gibbs 能量被故意丢掉</b>',
  en:'“Regulation is not free. Making a regulatory protein costs a transcription and a translation, and that is the price of the first level. Every covalent switch costs a phosphate group taken from ATP to put the switch on, and a hydrolysis by a phosphatase to take it off. One full cycle of that switch therefore costs one ATP. And the committed step costs the most. The cell makes that step irreversible by coupling it to ATP hydrolysis, and the Gibbs energy that pays for the irreversibility cannot be recovered.”',
  note:'<b>第 ③ 条是这张账的重点</b>，也是点 08 那个公式块的现金化：<code>−30,5 + 13,8 = −16,7</code>，<b>那 16,7 kJ/mol 就是「不回头」的标价</b>。<br>第 ② 条要小心措辞：磷酸化从 ATP 拿的是<b>磷酸基</b>，去磷酸化是<b>水解</b>掉它，<b>所以一轮下来净耗一个 ATP，磷酸并没有还回 ADP 上</b>。英文：“The phosphate is not handed back to ADP. The phosphatase hydrolyses it off, so one on-and-off cycle costs one ATP.”<br>⚠ <b>这一整格的框架是我搭的，书没有把它写成一笔账</b>；但三条的每一条都有书的出处（<span class="pg">p221</span> 诱导 · <span class="pg">p222 p063</span> 磷酸化 · <span class="pg">p131 p222</span> 不可逆）。被问「书上这么说的吗」，答：<b>「书分开给了这三件事，把它们当成一笔代价来看是我的说法。」</b>——<b>承认这一句比含糊过去稳得多。</b>'},
 {n:'13', t:'产出（买到了什么）',
  big:'<b>方向</b>（不可逆步只能一个方向）· <b>灵敏度</b>（效应物小变化 → 速率大变化）· <b>独立性</b>（分解和合成可以分开调）· <b>放大</b>（一个信号 → 很多个酶分子）',
  en:'“What it buys is four things. <b>Direction</b>, because an irreversible step runs one way only. <b>Sensitivity</b>, because a small change in an effector gives a large change in rate. The textbook makes that point twice, once for substrate concentration in the first-order part of the Michaelis curve, and once for the sigmoid curve of an allosteric enzyme. <b>Independence</b>, because catabolism and anabolism use different routes, different enzymes and different compartments, so one can be switched without the other. And <b>amplification</b>, because one hormone molecule raises many molecules of cyclic AMP, and the kinase then modifies many molecules of enzyme.”',
  note:'<b>「灵敏度」这一条书讲了两遍，而且两遍都很值得引。</b><br>① <b>Michaelis 那一段</b>（书把它当成<b>最简单的一种调控</b>，并且自己承认「不太有效」）：<i>a small change in substrate concentration causes a large change in the rate of the reaction … For a simple enzyme this dependence holds only in the region of first-order kinetics, in the linear part of the curve. At high substrate concentrations this regulation no longer applies.</i> <b>也就是说：底物浓度这条调控只在曲线的直线段有效，饱和了就失灵。</b>英文：“The textbook counts the plain enzyme reaction itself as a simple form of regulation, though it says not a very effective one. A small change in substrate concentration gives a large change in rate, but only in the first-order, linear part of the curve. At high substrate concentration that regulation no longer works.”<span class="pg">p221</span><br>② <b>变构那一段</b>：第一个底物结合会<b>提高</b>酶对后续底物的亲和力（<b>positive cooperativity</b>），所以在某个浓度区间里，底物浓度很小的变化会引起<b>比非调节酶大得多</b>的速率变化；曲线是 <b>S 形</b>而不是双曲线，<b>Michaelis-Menten 方程对它不成立</b>。<span class="pg">p063</span><br><b>这两条连起来说，等于把卡 02 的 Michaelis 曲线接进了调控题</b>——而且是书自己接的。'},
 {n:'14', t:'总结（净账）',
  big:'<b>细胞拿 Gibbs 能量买控制权。</b>接近平衡的步骤便宜但控不了；不可逆的定局步贵，但只有它能控。<b>所以控制点又少又固定</b>',
  en:'“So the net balance is this. The cell pays Gibbs energy and it buys control. Every step that stays near equilibrium is cheap and cannot be used for control. Every committed step is expensive and can be. That is why the control points are few, and why the textbook puts the controlling enzyme at the beginning of a sequence and at the practically irreversible reactions. If I had to compress the whole answer into one sentence, it is this. <b>Metabolic regulation is the cell spending free energy at a small number of irreversible steps in order to decide the direction and the rate of everything downstream of them.</b>”',
  note:'<b>最后那一句是整张卡的收口</b>，也是他听完以后会记住的那一句。<b>练到能一口气说完它。</b><br>⚠ 别说成「每条通路只有一个控制点」——书没有数过，而且糖原那一格里一条路上就有两个（synthase 和 phosphorylase）。<b>能说的是「少」和「在开头 ＋ 在不可逆步」，这两条书都有。</b><span class="pg">p222</span>'},

 {g:'激素 · 最外层', gn:'书 §10.3 紧接着 §10.2，<b>所以这题很可能一路问到这里</b>。四个机制记名字和第二信使就够，不要往内分泌学里走。'},
 {n:'15', t:'激素调控 ＋ 四种作用机制',
  big:'<b>特异性来自受体</b>（只有靶细胞有）· 四条：<b>腺苷酸环化酶级联</b> · <b>磷酸肌醇级联</b> · <b>受体酪氨酸激酶</b> · <b>类固醇/甲状腺激素直接进核</b>',
  en:'“The outermost layer is hormonal. A <b>hormone</b> is a specifically acting substance made in a specialised gland, tissue or cell, and released into the blood, or acting on the cells around it. Most hormones act only on certain target organs, tissues or cells, and the reason is that only target cells carry the receptor for that hormone. The textbook distinguishes four main mechanisms. First, the <b>adenylate cyclase cascade</b>. The hormone raises the level of cyclic AMP, and cyclic AMP activates a protein kinase, which then phosphorylates serine and threonine residues of the target proteins. Adrenaline and glucagon act this way. Second, the <b>phosphoinositide cascade</b>, named after the membrane lipid it starts from. The hormone activates phospholipase C, and that enzyme hydrolyses phosphatidylinositol-4,5-bisphosphate into two signalling substances. Inositol trisphosphate opens calcium channels, and diacylglycerol activates protein kinase C. Serotonin is the textbook’s example. Third, insulin and a number of growth factors stimulate the <b>tyrosine kinase</b> activity of their own receptors. The receptor first phosphorylates its own tyrosines, which raises its kinase activity towards the target proteins. Fourth, <b>steroid and thyroid hormones</b> act in a completely different way. They enter the cell and bind receptors that are DNA-binding proteins, those receptors carry them into the nucleus, and there they bind specific places in DNA and activate certain parts of a gene.”',
  note:'<b>「特异性来自受体」这一句要先说</b>，因为它是激素调控唯一的通则，其余四条都是细节。书 <span class="pg">p223</span>：<i>The cause of this specificity lies in the fact that only target cells contain receptors for the hormone in question.</i><br><b>书自己点了这个机制的两个「普遍意义」</b>（<span class="pg">p224</span>），这两句比四个机制的名字值钱：<br>① <b>通过形成第二信使产生放大效应</b>（<i>an amplifying effect through the formation of a second messenger</i>）。<br>② <b>同一个基本原理，不同细胞给出不同的回答</b>——取决于各自的受体装备和对 cAMP 敏感的酶系统（<i>different cells and tissues can be influenced in different ways on the same basic principle</i>）。<b>这一条是「为什么肾上腺素在肝和在肌肉做的事不一样」的正解。</b><br>③ 还有一条<b>关掉的机制</b>：cAMP 在细胞里被 <b>phosphodiesterase</b> 迅速降解成 AMP，<b>半衰期很短</b>。英文：“Cyclic AMP is quickly broken down in the cell to AMP by a <b>phosphodiesterase</b>, an enzyme that hydrolyses the cyclic ring open. The half-life of cyclic AMP is short. A signal that cannot be switched off is not a signal.”<br><b>激素分四类</b>（<span class="pg">p225</span>）：① 类固醇 ② 氨基酸衍生的 ③ 肽和蛋白质激素 ④ 二十烷类（eikosanoids，含 20 个碳，希腊文 eikosi ＝ 二十）。<b>最后那个词源书自己写了，说出来很像读过书的人。</b><br>⚠ <b>激素腺体自己也被激素调控</b>：书给的三级回路是<b>下丘脑神经分泌 → 垂体促腺激素 → 肾上腺皮质的 cortisol</b>，而 cortisol 回头<b>抑制</b>上游。<b>这就是反馈抑制，只是尺度从一条通路放大到了整个机体。</b><span class="pg">p222 p223</span>'},

 {g:'收尾', gn:'一句停住，一句伸出去。'},
 {n:'16', t:'落点',
  big:'三个层级 ＋ 一条位置的规矩，再往下就是<b>热力学</b>',
  en:'“So the principles come down to three levels and one rule. Control the amount of enzyme, control the activity of the enzyme you already have, or control whether the enzyme and the substrate ever meet. And put the control at the practically irreversible committed step. Below that we are asking why a reaction has a direction at all, and that is thermodynamics rather than metabolism.”',
  note:'落在<b>热力学</b>那块地基上，而且是这张卡最自然的落点，因为整张卡的骨架就是「方向是买来的」。<b>说完这句就停。</b>'},
 {n:'17', t:'留口子（落在她的主场）',
  big:'第一层级正是她天天在用的：<b>IPTG 诱导</b>、<b>GAL1 启动子</b>；而变构说到底是一个 <b>Kd</b> 的问题',
  en:'“One thing worth adding. The level I work with every day is the first one, the amount of protein. On my bacterial expression plasmids I induce with <b>IPTG</b>. That is the lac operon used as a tool. In yeast surface display I switch the library on with the <b>GAL1</b> promoter, so my protein appears only when I feed galactose. And allosteric control is really a binding question. An effector has its own <b>dissociation constant</b> for its own site, so the response of the enzyme to that effector is a binding curve. The sigmoid curve the textbook draws for an allosteric enzyme is that binding curve made steep by cooperativity.”',
  note:'<b>这是给他的抓手</b>：往「诱导」走会落到卡 12（lac / trp 操纵子，IPTG 为什么用 gratuitous inducer），往「变构是 Kd 问题」走会落到卡 02（Kd、半数占据、饱和曲线）。<b>两个方向都是她的主场，都接得住。</b><br><b>「S 形曲线就是被协同性变陡了的结合曲线」这一句要练熟</b>——它把这张卡、卡 02 和卡 18 一次接起来，而且是他最容易觉得「这人真的懂」的那种句子。'}
],
end:'<b>这题的成败在开口那二十秒</b>：你说的是「三个层级 ＋ 一条位置的规矩」，还是「糖酵解这样调、循环那样调」。<br>后者是目录，每报一条通路就递给他一把刀；前者是规则，每个例子都变成你的证据。<b>记住例子挂在哪张卡上（glycolysis → gly · citric acid cycle → 24 · respiratory chain → 25 · operons → 12 · Kd → 02），报名字就好，不要展开。</b>',

/* ---------------- 演练 ---------------- */
sib:[
['<b>定义</b>：metabolism ＝ 细胞里一整套<b>严格协同</b>的酶反应；regulation ＝ 设定它们的速率；<b>principles ＝ 规则，不是通路清单</b>',
 '“Metabolism is the whole set of strictly coordinated enzyme reactions in a living cell. Regulation means setting their rates. By principles I mean the general rules about where control sits, not a list of pathways.”',
 '<b>第一句就把答案的形状定下来</b>，他才不会追着你要通路名。书 p218 的原定义。'],
['<b>为什么要调</b>：不控制的话反应会<b>白跑</b>，酶系统还会<b>互相抵消</b>',
 '“Under uncontrolled coexistence, enzyme reactions would run uselessly in many places, and some enzyme systems would cancel each other out.”',
 '书 §10.2 的第一句，几乎可以照搬。<b>「互相抵消」是后面互控那一格的种子。</b>'],
['<b>层级一 · 数量</b>：induction / repression；书说这是动用<b>遗传装备</b>的层级。慢，改的是上限',
 '“The first level is the amount of enzyme, by induction or repression. The textbook says this is the level at which the cell uses its genetic equipment.”',
 '例子：lac 和 trp 操纵子（卡 12），类固醇激素诱导酶的合成。'],
['<b>层级二 · 活性</b>：① 变构 ② 共价修饰 ③ 抑制剂结合进活性部位。快，因为不用造东西',
 '“The second level is the activity of enzymes that already exist. Allosteric control, covalent modification, and binding of an inhibitor into the active site.”',
 '<b>三条要分开报。</b>分别在共价与否、花不花 ATP、可不可逆上不同。'],
['<b>层级三 · 可及性</b>：<b>compartmentation</b> ＋ <b>limiting metabolite</b>',
 '“The third level is access. Compartmentation, and regulation by limiting metabolites. The textbook’s example is phosphorylation in the respiratory chain, where the limiting factor is enough ADP.”',
 '<b>ADP 那个例子把调控接回了 #25 #26</b>：细胞不缺燃料时缺的是空电池。'],
['<b>位置的规矩</b>：控在<b>实际上不可逆</b>的那一步（强放能的），例子 <b>phosphofructokinase</b>',
 '“A controlling enzyme can do its job by catalysing a practically irreversible reaction, for example a strongly exergonic one. The textbook’s example is phosphofructokinase.”',
 '<b>这一句是全卡最值钱的。</b>书同页还说 PFK 的活性由 <b>ATP/ADP 系统</b>变构控制——位置和手段叠在同一个酶上。'],
['<b>反过来</b>：接近平衡的那一步<b>控了也没用</b>，酶只改变到达平衡的快慢',
 '“Control at a step near equilibrium would achieve nothing. Such an enzyme only speeds up the approach to an equilibrium whose position is already fixed.”',
 '证据是书自己印的三个平衡组成（30% · 89/11% · 96%，p164）。<b>用书的一半打书的另一半。</b>'],
['<b>反馈抑制</b>：终产物回头抑制<b>第一个</b>酶，那个酶是变构酶',
 '“Only the end product acts on the first enzyme of the sequence, and that first enzyme is allosteric. The textbook calls it the controlling enzyme.”',
 '为什么是第一个：<b>书自己给了理由</b>——这样把碳、氮和能量的消耗降到最低。例子 Thr → Ile；还有 ATCase 被 CTP 抑制（p072）。'],
['<b>互控</b>：糖原合成酶活的时候磷酸化酶失活，反过来也一样。<b>同一个激酶，两个相反的答案</b>',
 '“Glycogen synthase is active when phosphorylase is inactive, and the other way round. The same protein kinase phosphorylates both, inactivating the synthase and activating the phosphorylase.”',
 '书 p174 原话叫 <b>coordinately controlled by an amplifying cascade</b>。这就是它防止「互相抵消」的办法。'],
['<b>前提</b>：分解和合成<b>不是同一条路倒着走</b>——不同的路、不同的酶、不同的区室，<b>所以才能分开调</b>',
 '“Catabolic and anabolic routes are not the reverse of one another. Because they are separate, they can be regulated independently.”',
 '书 p218 自己下的结论，p134 又说了一遍。<b>没有这一条，前面三个层级都没有落脚点。</b>'],
['<b>账</b>：投入 ＝ 造调节蛋白 ＋ 每次开关一个 ATP ＋ <b>不可逆步丢掉的 Gibbs 能量</b>；产出 ＝ 方向 · 灵敏度 · 独立性 · 放大',
 '“Regulation is not free. The cell pays Gibbs energy and buys control.”',
 '<b>只说好处是半张账。</b>他一句「那代价呢」就能把你问停。'],
['<b>激素</b>：特异性来自<b>受体</b>；四个机制：cAMP 级联 · 磷酸肌醇级联 · 受体酪氨酸激酶 · 类固醇进核',
 '“Only target cells carry the receptor, and that is where the specificity comes from. The textbook distinguishes four main mechanisms of hormone action.”',
 '<b>最值钱的是书自己点的两个普遍意义</b>：第二信使带来<b>放大</b>，以及同一原理在不同细胞给出不同回答。'],
['<b>收口</b>：调控 ＝ 在<b>少数几个不可逆步</b>上花自由能，买下游全部的方向和速率',
 '“Metabolic regulation is the cell spending free energy at a small number of irreversible steps in order to decide the direction and the rate of everything downstream of them.”',
 '<b>练到能一口气说完。</b>这是他听完会记住的那一句。']
],

segs:[
 {tag:'段 1 · 定义', h:'三个实词各交代一句，并且声明这是规则不是清单',
  p:['“Metabolism is the whole set of strictly coordinated enzyme reactions that run in a living cell. The textbook divides it in two. <b>Catabolism</b> is the degrading half. It takes nutrients apart and releases energy. <b>Anabolism</b> is the synthetic half. It builds the cell’s own molecules and spends energy.',
     'Regulation means setting the rate of those reactions. So the cell makes what it needs, and not what it does not need. By <b>principles</b> I mean the general rules about where control sits and how it acts. I do not mean a list of pathways.',
     'The textbook gives the reason at the start of the chapter. Under uncontrolled coexistence, enzyme reactions in many places of metabolism would run uselessly, and the effects of some enzyme systems would cancel each other out. A high degree of organisation is possible only under consistent regulation.”'],
  note:'<b>「I do not mean a list of pathways」这一句请务必说出口。</b>它把接下来六十秒的结构交给了你，而不是交给他。<br>三个实词都交代了：<b>metabolic</b>（那一整套酶反应，分解 ＋ 合成）· <b>regulation</b>（设定速率）· <b>principles</b>（规则）。'},
 {tag:'段 2 · general', h:'三个层级 ＋ 一条位置的规矩，这一屏就是全部答案的骨架',
  p:['“The textbook says regulation can be considered at many levels, from the whole organism down to a single enzyme reaction, and physiologically it distinguishes nervous from hormonal control. I will stay at the enzyme level, which is where the general rules live.',
     'There are three levels at which a cell controls flux. Flux means the rate at which material passes through a pathway. The first level is the <b>amount</b> of enzyme, controlled by induction or repression of its synthesis. That is slow, and it changes the ceiling.',
     'The second level is the <b>activity</b> of enzyme molecules that already exist. That is fast, because nothing has to be made. It happens in three ways: allosteric control, covalent modification, and the binding of an inhibitor into the active site.',
     'The third level is <b>access</b>. Compartmentation decides whether the enzyme and the substrate are even in the same space. The supply of a limiting metabolite decides whether there is anything to work on.',
     'And there is one rule about <b>position</b>. Control sits at a practically irreversible step, near the beginning of the pathway.”'],
  note:'<b>这一屏说完，这题其实已经答完了。</b>后面两段都是证据。<br><b>flux 这个词在整套卡里只有这张用，所以要就地交代</b>（「物质通过一条通路的速率」）——这是她自己定的规矩：别在一张卡里用另一张卡没解释过的词。'},
 {tag:'段 3 · 稍展开', h:'每条规则挂一个书里的实例，报名字，不要展开',
  p:['“For the first level the textbook’s examples are the <b>lac and trp operons</b>, and it adds that steroid hormones induce enzyme biosynthesis.',
     'For allosteric control the classic pattern is <b>feedback inhibition</b>. The end product of a sequence inhibits the first enzyme of that sequence, and that enzyme is allosteric. The textbook calls it the <b>controlling enzyme</b>. Its examples are the synthesis of isoleucine from threonine, and aspartate transcarbamoylase inhibited by CTP. It says this minimises the demand for carbon, nitrogen and energy.',
     'For covalent modification the example is <b>glycogen phosphorylase</b>, switched between an active and an almost inactive form by reversible phosphorylation of a serine. The irreversible kind is a proenzyme cut into an active enzyme, trypsinogen into trypsin.',
     'For the rule about position the example is <b>phosphofructokinase</b>. It catalyses a practically irreversible phosphorylation, which makes it the rate-limiting reaction of glycolysis, and its activity is allosterically controlled by the ATP and ADP system. Position and mechanism sit on the same enzyme.',
     'And regulating two opposing pathways is different from regulating one enzyme. Glycogen synthesis and breakdown are coordinately controlled by an amplifying cascade. The synthase is active when the phosphorylase is inactive, and the other way round.”'],
  note:'<b>时间不够就砍到三句</b>：反馈抑制、phosphofructokinase、糖原互控。<b>不要砍掉「position and mechanism sit on the same enzyme」</b>——那一句是这段的收口。<br><b>每个例子只报名字。</b>糖酵解展开在卡 gly，柠檬酸循环在卡 24，呼吸链在卡 25，操纵子在卡 12。他要哪个你再打开哪个。'},
 {tag:'段 4 · 留口子', h:'伸到她的主场：诱导表达 ＋ 变构其实是 Kd',
  p:['“One thing worth adding. The level I work with every day is the first one, the amount of protein. On my bacterial expression plasmids I induce with <b>IPTG</b>, which is the lac operon used as a laboratory tool. In yeast surface display I switch the library on with the <b>GAL1</b> promoter, so my protein appears only when I feed galactose.',
     'And allosteric control is really a binding question. An effector has its own <b>dissociation constant</b> for its own site. So the response of an enzyme to that effector is a binding curve, and the sigmoid curve the textbook draws for an allosteric enzyme is that binding curve made steep by cooperativity.”'],
  note:'<b>两个口子都落在她接得住的地方</b>：诱导 → 卡 12（IPTG 为什么用 gratuitous inducer、GAL1）；变构 → 卡 02（Kd、半数占据、饱和曲线）。<br>⚠ <b>不要在这一段主动提 Michaelis-Menten 方程对变构酶不成立</b>——那是加分项，留到他追问再给（见追问组）。<b>答八分就停。</b>'}
],

why:{
 rungs:[
  ['为什么代谢一定要被调控？','因为活细胞是<b>开放系统</b>，处在<b>动态平衡</b>（书也叫稳态）而不是静态平衡（书 p130）。它必须不断跟环境交换物质和能量，而同一批中间产物同时被多条路用。不控制的话，酶反应会在很多地方白跑，有些酶系统还会<b>互相抵消</b>（书 p221）。'],
  ['那为什么控制点总是落在<b>不可逆</b>的那一步？','因为只有不可逆的步骤决定<b>流向</b>。接近平衡的步骤，酶改变的只是<b>到达平衡的快慢</b>，平衡的位置由热力学定死——书自己把三个平衡组成印出来了（30% · 89/11% · 96%，p164）。<b>阀门只能装在水不会倒流的地方。</b>'],
  ['那一步为什么会不可逆？','因为它<b>跟 ATP 水解偶联</b>，两步的 Gibbs 能变<b>相加</b>之后是负的。书 p131 的原例：<code>+13,8</code>（糖被磷酸化，自己不会走）加 <code>−30,5</code>（ATP 水解，自己会走）等于 <code>−16,7</code>。<b>不可逆不是化学性质，是一笔付过钱的性质。</b>']
 ],
 stop:{lbl:'停 · 落在热力学',
  say:'“Below that we are asking why a reaction has a direction at all, and why free energy cannot be recovered once it is spent. That is thermodynamics rather than metabolic regulation.”'}
},

/* ---------------- 图 ---------------- */
figs:[
 {k:'认',
  src:'img/p063_obr37_allosteric_sigmoid.jpg',
  t:'S 形曲线 —— 变构酶被抑制和被活化（书 Obr. 3.7）',
  cap:'<b>这张是「认」，而且它是这张卡最值钱的一张。</b>他把它摆过来问「这是什么」，<b>八分答案一句话</b>：'
    + '<br>“An allosteric enzyme. The curve is <b>sigmoid</b>, not hyperbolic, because of cooperativity. The two outer curves are the same enzyme with an inhibitor and with an activator.”'
    + '<br><br><b>图上只有四样东西</b>：纵轴 <b>v</b>，横轴 <b>[A]</b>（底物浓度），纵轴上标出 <b>V<sub>lim</sub>/2</b>，横轴上标出 <b>S₀,₅</b>；三条曲线，右边那条标 <b>1</b>，左边那条标 <b>2</b>，中间那条不标号——<b>中间那条是没加东西的原样</b>。'
    + '<br><b>1 ＝ 抑制</b>：曲线<b>右移</b>，要更多底物才到半速。<b>2 ＝ 活化</b>：曲线<b>左移</b>，更少底物就到半速。'
    + '<br><br>⚠⚠ <b>这张图上没有 Km，只有 S₀,₅。</b>这不是印错——<b>变构酶不服从 Michaelis-Menten，所以「Km」这个符号在这里根本不成立</b>，书换了一个只表示「半速时的底物浓度」的记号。<b>他指着横轴问「这是不是 Km」，答 “No.”，然后给理由。</b>'
    + '<br><br><b>S 形曲线的意义，一句话</b>：双曲线在任何浓度上都是「加一点底物、快一点点」；<b>S 形有一段是「加一点底物、快很多」——那一段就是可调的工作区</b>。<b>调控酶要的不是快，是陡。</b>'
    + '<br><b>被追问就给这一层</b>：“Binding the first substrate molecule raises the affinity for the next ones. That is positive cooperativity. So over a narrow range of concentration a small change in substrate gives a much larger change in rate. Negative cooperativity is the opposite, and then the rate rises only very slowly.”'
    + '<br><br><b>这张图把卡 02 接进了调控题，而且是书自己接的</b>——同一本书的第 56 页画双曲线，第 63 页画这一条，<b>两张图放一起就是「调控酶跟普通酶不是一回事」的全部证据</b>。'
    + '<br>⚠ 图下那行捷克图注写的就是「变构酶的动力学行为：1 — 抑制，2 — 活化」，<b>已经裁在框外，不用读</b>。',
  src2:'Biochemie, Obr. 3.7, p063 · 400 dpi 原图裁切，未修改（捷克图注在框外）'},

 {k:'画',
  src:'img/p062_feedback_inhibition.jpg',
  t:'反馈抑制 —— 三笔画完的那张小图（书 p062）',
  cap:'<b>这张是「画」，而且它是这道题里最容易得分的一笔</b>：三秒画完，说的却是整条原则。'
    + '<br><b>图上就一行</b>：<code>A —E₁→ B —E₂→ C —E₃→ D —E₄→ E —E₅→ F</code>，然后<b>从最右边的 F 引一条长线绕回左边，指回第一根箭</b>。'
    + '<br><br><b>画的顺序（三步，边画边说）</b>：'
    + '<br>① 横着写 <b>A B C D E F</b> 六个字母，五根箭连起来，<b>每根箭上标 E₁ 到 E₅</b>。'
    + '<br>② 从 <b>F</b> 往下拉一条线，向左绕回去，<b>指回 A→B 那根箭</b>。'
    + '<br>③ 在 <b>E₁</b> 底下写 <b>allosteric</b>，在回线的末端画一个<b>横杠</b>表示抑制。'
    + '<br><b>边画边说的两句</b>：“The end product of the sequence inhibits the <b>first</b> enzyme of the sequence, and that enzyme is allosteric.”'
    + '<br>“Inhibiting the last enzyme would stop the product but leave every intermediate already built. Inhibiting the first means nothing is spent at all. The textbook says this minimises the demand for carbon, nitrogen and energy.”'
    + '<br><br>⚠ <b>一处跟速背 06 不一样的细节，先知道</b>：<b>书这张图的回线末端画的是一个普通箭头，不是横杠。</b>横杠是通用的抑制记号，画上去更清楚、也不会被说错；<b>但你要是照书临摹，看到的是箭头。</b>两种都行，说清楚「这一条是抑制」就够。'
    + '<br><br><b>书还给了第二个实例，可以顺手接上</b>：嘧啶核苷酸合成被控在第一步，<b>aspartate transcarbamoylase</b> 是变构酶，被整条路的终产物 <b>CTP</b> 抑制（<span class="pg">p072</span>）。<b>画完这张图再报这个名字，等于把规则和例子一起交了。</b>',
  src2:'Biochemie, p062 · 400 dpi 原图裁切，未修改'},

 {k:'画',
  src:'img/p219_obr101_acetylcoa_hub.jpg',
  t:'acetyl-CoA 是个漏斗 —— 三进三出（书 Obr. 10.1）',
  cap:'<b>这张是「画」，而且它十秒画完。</b>它回答的是「代谢池 / 节点是什么意思」——<b>节点就是很多条路都经过的那个点</b>。'
    + '<br><br><b>图上三行</b>：最上一行三个词 <b>糖类 · 脂类 · 蛋白质</b>；第二行对应 <b>pyruvate · 脂肪酸 · 氨基酸</b>；三根箭往下汇到中间一个圈，圈里写 <b>Acetyl-CoA</b>；再从圈往下发三根箭到 <b>柠檬酸循环 ＋ 呼吸链</b>、<b>脂类</b>、<b>异戊二烯类</b>。'
    + '<br><b>这些词的捷克拼法跟国际写法几乎一样</b>，中间那个圈更是直接印着 <b>Acetyl-CoA</b>，<b>整张图不用读捷克语也看得懂</b>。'
    + '<br><br><b>画的顺序（三步，边画边说）</b>：'
    + '<br>① 中间画一个<b>椭圆</b>，里面写 <b>acetyl-CoA</b>。'
    + '<br>② 上面写三个来路：<b>carbohydrates → pyruvate</b>、<b>lipids → fatty acids</b>、<b>proteins → amino acids</b>，三根箭朝下汇进椭圆。'
    + '<br>③ 下面写三个去路：<b>citric acid cycle ＋ respiratory chain</b>、<b>lipids</b>、<b>isoprenoids</b>，三根箭从椭圆朝下发出去。'
    + '<br><b>边画边说的两句</b>：“Acetyl-CoA is the textbook’s worked example of a metabolic pool. It is made by the breakdown of sugars, lipids and proteins, and it is drawn off for the citric acid cycle, for fatty acids and lipids, and for isoprenoids and steroids.”'
    + '<br>“Regulating a pathway really means holding the level of a node like this one where it is wanted. The concentration is low, of the order of millimoles per litre, but the turnover is very fast.”'
    + '<br><br><b>书点名的调控节点一共五个</b>：<b>acetyl-CoA · pyruvate · 2-oxoglutarate · NADH 和 NADPH · ATP</b>。<b>这张图只画了第一个，另外四个报名字就行</b>（<span class="pg">p218 p219</span>）。'
    + '<br>⚠ <b>注意「脂类」在图上出现了两次</b>——上面一次是来路，下面一次是去路。<b>这不是印错，这正是漏斗的意思</b>：同一类物质拆进来又装回去，中间过的是同一个池子。',
  src2:'Biochemie, Obr. 10.1, p219 · 350 dpi 原图裁切，未修改（捷克图注在框外）'},

 {k:'认',
  src:'img/p175_glycogen_cascade.jpg',
  t:'糖原磷酸化酶的放大级联 —— 共价修饰那一层长什么样（书 p175）',
  cap:'<b>这张是「认」，它是三个层级里「共价修饰」那一层的实物图。</b>他摆出来问「这是什么」，答：'
    + '<br>“The amplifying cascade that controls glycogen breakdown and glycogen synthesis at the same time. Every step is a phosphorylation, and one hormone molecule ends up switching a very large number of enzyme molecules.”'
    + '<br><br><b>从右上角往下读，一条主线</b>：<b>adrenalin / glukagon</b> 两个激素名印在最右上 → 激活 <b>adenylate cyclase</b> → 把 <b>ATP</b> 变成 <b>3′,5′-cAMP</b>（<b>这个记号图上直接印着</b>）→ 激活底下那条长条里的 <b>protein kinase</b> → 它分两路：'
    + '<br>① <b>往左</b>：把 <b>phosphorylase kinase b</b> 磷酸化成 <b>a</b>（花 ATP），a 再把 <b>phosphorylase b</b> 磷酸化成 <b>a</b> —— <b>phosphorylase a 才是能拆糖原的那个</b>，图中间写着 <b>glycogen → glucose-1-phosphate</b>。'
    + '<br>② <b>往右</b>：把 <b>glycogen synthase I</b> 磷酸化成 <b>D</b> —— <b>磷酸化在这一侧是关掉</b>。'
    + '<br><b>最下面那条长条是 protein phosphatase</b>，它把所有磷酸拆下来（图上那些圈住的 P），<b>把两边同时拨回原位</b>。'
    + '<br><br>⚠⚠ <b>这张图的全部要害在「同一个信号，两个相反的结果」</b>：<b>磷酸化让分解的那个酶开、让合成的那个酶关</b>。'
    + '<br><b>这句话要背下来</b>：“One and the same phosphorylation activates the degrading enzyme and inactivates the synthesising one. That is how the cell avoids running both directions at once, which would only burn ATP.”'
    + '<br><br><b>三个可以主动留的口子</b>：① <b>放大</b>——每一级都是一个酶去改很多个下一级，所以叫 amplifying cascade。② <b>可逆</b>——磷酸酶把它全拆回去，所以信号会停。③ <b>a 和 b 这两个字母</b>：a 是磷酸化的活性形式，b 是去磷酸的；<b>合成酶那一侧反过来用 I 和 D</b>。'
    + '<br>⚠ 图上的酶名用的是书的语言，但 <b>adenylate cyclase · protein kinase · phosphorylase · glycogen synthase · protein phosphatase</b> 这五个跟图上印的只差词尾，<b>位置＋首几个字母就足够认出来</b>。',
  src2:'Biochemie, p175（无编号图）· 250 dpi 原图裁切，未修改'},

 {k:'认',
  src:'img/p223_hormone_axis_feedback.jpg',
  t:'激素轴 —— 反馈抑制在整只动物身上是什么样（书 p223）',
  cap:'<b>这张是「认」，而且它跟那张 A→F 的小图是同一条原则的两个尺度。</b>'
    + '<br><b>图上从上往下五个圆角框</b>：<b>间脑 → 垂体 → 激素腺（肾上腺皮质）→ 外周起作用的激素（图上举的例子是 cortisol）→ 组织</b>。两根箭之间还标着中间产物：第一段写<b>神经分泌物</b>，第二段写<b>促腺激素</b>。'
    + '<br><b>左边那条竖线标着「抑制」两个字</b>，<b>它从最下面的激素引出来，同时指回垂体和间脑</b>——<b>终产物回头关掉上游</b>。'
    + '<br><br><b>看到图要说的那一段</b>：“This is feedback inhibition again, but on the level of the whole organism. The end product is the hormone itself, and it inhibits the two glands above it. It is the same principle as the end product of a pathway inhibiting the first enzyme.”'
    + '<br><br><b>为什么这张图值得单独记</b>：这张卡说的是<b>三个层级</b>，而这一张<b>把最上面那一层画成了图</b>。<b>拿它跟 p062 那张 A→F 并排</b>，你就能说一句考官很难接下去的话：'
    + '<br>“The same control shape appears at three scales: one enzyme inhibited by its own end product, one pathway inhibited by its end product, and one gland inhibited by the hormone it caused to be made.”'
    + '<br><br>⚠ <b>器官名是捷克语，但位置就是答案</b>：<b>从上往下是脑、垂体、腺体、激素、组织</b>，记这个顺序比记拼写有用。图上举的那个激素名跟英文只差一个字母。'
    + '<br>⚠ <b>别把这张图讲成内分泌学。</b>这道题问的是<b>代谢调控的原则</b>，这张图在卡上的身份是<b>「反馈抑制」这条原则的第三个例子</b>，说完就停（LOGIC §12：答八分）。',
  src2:'Biochemie, p223（激素调节示意图）· 400 dpi 原图裁切，未修改'},

 {k:'认',
  src:'img/p223_atp_camp_amp.jpg',
  t:'ATP → cyclic AMP → AMP —— 两个酶名就印在箭头上（书 p223）',
  cap:'<b>这张是「认」。</b>它三格竖着排，<b>要看的不是结构，是两根箭上的两个酶名</b>。'
    + '<br><b>最上面 ATP</b>：三个磷酸串成一条。<b>往下一根箭，箭旁印着 adenylate cyclase</b>，箭上甩出 <b>PPᵢ</b>（焦磷酸）。'
    + '<br><b>中间 cyclic AMP</b>：<b>只剩一个磷酸，而且它两头都接在同一个核糖上</b>——<b>这就是「环」的意思，图上画得很清楚</b>。'
    + '<br><b>再往下一根箭，箭旁印着 phosphodiesterase</b>，进 <b>H₂O</b>、出 <b>H⁺</b>。'
    + '<br><b>最下面 AMP</b>：环开了，磷酸只剩一头连着。'
    + '<br><br><b>看到图要说的那一段</b>：“Adenylate cyclase makes cyclic AMP from ATP and releases pyrophosphate. The cyclic form is a ring closed onto the ribose of the same nucleotide. A phosphodiesterase then hydrolyses it back to AMP, so the signal decays. That is why the response stops when the hormone is gone.”'
    + '<br><br>⚠ <b>一处可以把卡上的保留去掉</b>：速背里写着「3′ 和 5′ 那个环的连接方式书上有但 OCR 读不出」。<b>回原图看，同一章 <span class="pg">p175</span> 的级联图上直接印着 3′,5′-cAMP 这个记号</b>——<b>所以这个数字是书给的，可以放心报</b>，不必只说 “a ring closed onto the ribose”。'
    + '<br><br><b>这一格的落点</b>：<b>第二信使这个概念，全部好处就在「一个进不来的分子，靠一个小分子在里面代它说话」</b>。'
    + '<br><b>被追问就给这一句</b>：“The hormone never enters the cell. It binds a receptor on the outside, and cyclic AMP carries the message inside. One hormone molecule raises many molecules of cyclic AMP, and each of those switches on a kinase, so the signal is amplified at every step.”'
    + '<br>⚠ <b>结构本身不用背</b>，她另有专门的 app。这张图在卡上的身份是<b>「两个酶名 ＋ 一个环」</b>。',
  src2:'Biochemie, p223（ATP / 环化 AMP / AMP）· 400 dpi 原图裁切，未修改'}
],

/* ---------------- 同一个答案，不同问法 ---------------- */
recog:[
{q:'Metabolic regulation.',
 mean:'<b>他嘴里那句比卷面短得多。</b>卷面写 “What are the principles of metabolic regulation?”，口试他多半只说这两个词，然后等你自己搭结构。<b>所以答案要自带结构</b>：定义 → 为什么要调 → 三个层级 → 控在哪一步。',
 say:'“Metabolism is the whole set of strictly coordinated enzyme reactions in a living cell, and regulation means setting their rates. Without it, reactions would run uselessly and some enzyme systems would cancel each other out. A cell controls flux at three levels. The amount of enzyme, by induction or repression of its synthesis. The activity of the enzymes it already has, by allosteric control, by covalent modification, or by an inhibitor binding in the active site. And access, by compartmentation and by the supply of limiting metabolites. And there is one rule about position. Control sits at a practically irreversible step near the beginning of the pathway, because only there can the cell set the direction.”'},

{q:'Give me the principles, not the pathways.',
 mean:'<b>卷面上 “principles” 这个词就是他准备挖的方向。</b>他在测你会不会把目录当成理解。<b>这一行的存在本身就是提醒你：不要报通路。</b>',
 say:'“The principles are these. One, regulation acts on three things: how much enzyme there is, how active the enzyme already there is, and whether enzyme and substrate can meet. Two, control sits at practically irreversible steps, because a step near equilibrium cannot set a direction. Three, control sits near the beginning of a sequence, because that is what minimises waste. Four, catabolism and anabolism run by separate routes with separate enzymes in separate compartments, and that separation is what makes independent regulation possible at all. The pathways are only where I would go for examples.”'},

{q:'What does metabolic mean here?',
 mean:'<b>他默认你知道，所以这一刀来得很快。</b>用书的定义答，一句就够。',
 say:'“Metabolism, or more precisely intermediary metabolism, is the sum of strictly mutually coordinated reactions that run in a living cell. The degrading part is called <b>catabolism</b> and the sum of the synthetic reactions is <b>anabolism</b>. The textbook lists four functions. Getting chemical energy out of nutrients or out of sunlight. Converting external substances into building blocks or precursors. Making the cell’s own proteins, nucleic acids and lipids out of those precursors. And making and breaking down the molecules a cell needs for its specific functions.”'},

{q:'What is regulation, in one sentence?',
 mean:'要的是<b>一句</b>。别趁机把三个层级倒出来。',
 say:'“Regulation is setting the rate of an enzyme reaction, or of a system of enzyme reactions, so that the cell makes what it needs at the moment it needs it.”'},

{q:'Where in a pathway is a pathway regulated?',
 mean:'<b>这一刀问的是位置，不是手段。</b>答案是书里标题叫 <i>the controlling reaction</i> 的那一节。',
 say:'“At a practically irreversible step, and usually near the beginning of the sequence. The textbook says a controlling enzyme does its job either by being allosterically controlled or by catalysing a practically irreversible reaction, for example a strongly exergonic one. Its example is phosphofructokinase, whose phosphorylation of fructose-6-phosphate is practically irreversible and is therefore the rate-limiting reaction of glycolysis. Near the beginning, because inhibiting the last enzyme would leave all the intermediates already built, while inhibiting the first spends nothing.”'},

{q:'How does a cell regulate an enzyme it has already made?',
 mean:'<b>只问第二层级。</b>三条分开报，然后停。',
 say:'“Three ways. First, <b>allosterically</b>. A metabolite binds at a site other than the active site and changes the conformation. The textbook calls allosteric control one of the most effective mechanisms of metabolic regulation. Second, by <b>covalent modification</b>, most often phosphorylation carried out by another enzyme. The irreversible version of that is a proenzyme cut into an active enzyme. Third, by <b>inhibitor binding</b> into the active site. The product of the reaction can do this itself, so the reaction slows down while its product piles up.”'},

{q:'What is the difference between regulating one enzyme and regulating two opposing pathways?',
 mean:'问的是 <b>reciprocal regulation</b>。书的糖原例子是现成的。',
 say:'“Regulating one enzyme only changes one rate. Regulating two opposing pathways has to change two rates in opposite directions at the same time, otherwise the cell builds and destroys the same molecule and turns ATP into heat. The textbook’s case is glycogen. Synthesis and breakdown are coordinately controlled by an amplifying cascade, and glycogen synthase is active when phosphorylase is inactive. Adrenaline and glucagon raise cyclic AMP, cyclic AMP activates one protein kinase, and that same kinase inactivates the synthase and activates the phosphorylase. One signal, one enzyme, two opposite answers.”'},

{q:'Does the textbook regulate photosynthesis anywhere?',
 mean:'<b>这是一个陷阱方向。</b>书第 9 章<b>没有</b>调控一节，所以不要现编。<b>先说书里没有，再给书里确实有的那一条。</b>',
 say:'“Not as a separate section. What the textbook does give is the division of the two reducing currencies. The NADPH made by the pentose phosphate pathway is not reoxidised in the respiratory chain but used for synthetic reactions, and in fatty acid metabolism the hydrogen donor for synthesis is NADPH while oxidation produces NADH. Keeping two separate pools of reducing power is itself a regulatory principle, because it lets reduction and oxidation run at the same time in the same cell.”',
 tail:'⚠ <b>我 grep 过第 9 章全部页面（p198–p217），没有 regulation / inhibition / control 这三个词的实质出现（是按书本身的语言查的）</b>。所以「书没有单独讲」这句话是查过的，可以放心说。<b>说「这本书里没有」之前先 grep 全书，这条是 LOGIC §18 的教训。</b>'},

{q:'Why does a diabetic’s breath smell of acetone?', odd:1,
 mean:'<b>听起来是临床题，其实是调控失灵题。</b>而且答案就印在书 p220 上，正好在这一章。',
 say:'“Because the regulation has failed at the acetyl-CoA node. The textbook says that when the organism is flooded with acetyl-CoA, for example in diabetes, two of its molecules can give acetoacetic acid and acetone. Acetyl-CoA is the central node where sugar, fat and most amino acids all arrive. Normally the cell either oxidises it in the citric acid cycle when it needs energy, or uses it for fatty acid synthesis when energy demand is low. When neither exit can take it, it overflows into ketone bodies, and acetone is volatile, so it leaves in the breath.”',
 tail:'书 <span class="pg">p220</span> 原话：<i>When the organism is flooded with acetyl-CoA (for example in diabetes), acetoacetic acid and acetone can arise from two of its molecules.</i><br><b>「acetone 挥发所以呼出来」这半句书没有，是通用常识</b>——但它不是数字，也不会被证伪，说出来没有风险。<b>「两个出口」那一句在同页</b>：需要能量时 acetyl-CoA 进循环，能量需求低时用去合成脂肪酸。'},

{q:'Why does coffee keep you awake?', odd:1,
 mean:'<b>入口完全不像生化题</b>，答案却是这一章的第二信使那一格。',
 say:'“Because it interferes with the switching-off of a second messenger. The textbook says cyclic AMP is quickly broken down in the cell to AMP by a <b>phosphodiesterase</b>, an enzyme that hydrolyses the cyclic ring open. It says the half-life of cyclic AMP is short. Caffeine inhibits that phosphodiesterase, so cyclic AMP is not cleared and the hormone signal keeps running. The general point is that a signalling system needs a way to be switched off, and not only a way to be switched on.”',
 tail:'⚠ <b>「咖啡因抑制磷酸二酯酶」这本书里 0 命中，是通用知识</b>，不是这本书的说法。<b>报的时候带一句「that part is general, not from this textbook」</b>——书给的只有 phosphodiesterase 把 cAMP 降解成 AMP、半衰期短这两条（<span class="pg">p223 p224</span>）。<br><b>真正的得分点不是咖啡因，是最后那一句「信号必须能被关掉」。</b>'},

{q:'Your body makes about seventy kilograms of ATP a day. Where is it all?', odd:1,
 mean:'<b>书自己印了这个数</b>（p219），而它要考的是 <b>pool</b> 这个概念：重要的不是浓度，是周转。',
 say:'“It is the same small amount of ATP being recharged over and over. The textbook says the concentration of such a metabolite can be quite low, of the order of millimoles per litre, but its turnover is very fast. It calls that mobilisable store a <b>pool</b>, and it says what matters is not a high concentration but the ability of the organism to hold the level where it is wanted. So seventy kilograms is not a stock. It is a flow through a very small stock.”',
 tail:'<b>=== 公式和推导（背之前先看懂这一段） ===</b><br><b>式子</b>：<code>每分钟产量 = 70 kg ÷ 24 h ÷ 60 min</code>。<b>70 kg 和「mmol/l 量级」这两个数都是书 <span class="pg">p219</span> 印的</b>，下面只是除法。<br><br><b>三步算</b>（他说「给个数」的时候照这个报）：<br>① 书印的（英译）：<i>the human body makes about 70 kg of ATP in 24 hours</i>。<br>② <code>70 ÷ 24 ≈ 2,9 kg / 小时</code>。<br>③ <code>2 900 ÷ 60 ≈ 48 g / 分钟</code>。<br><br><br><b>=== 一句话理解 ===</b><br><b>这个数唯一的用处是让你说出「它不是库存，是流量」。</b>同一段书紧接着说：浓度可以相当低（mmol/l 量级），但周转非常快，<b>重要的是机体能不能把它的水平维持在需要的高度</b>。<br>所以 <b>pool 不是「有多少」，是「维持得住多少」</b>——一个水池的意义在于水位稳定，不在于池子大。<b>这正是「调控」这两个字在代谢里的物理含义：把某几个关键中间产物的水位摁住。</b>书点名的关键代谢物有五个：<b>acetyl-CoA · pyruvate · 2-oxoglutarate · 还原型烟酰胺辅酶（NADH 和 NADPH）· ATP</b>。<span class="pg">p218 p219</span><br>⚠ <b>体内 ATP 总量是多少书没有印，所以不要去算「一天循环多少次」</b>——那个数会当场被要求出处。'},

{q:'Why does adrenaline work within seconds, while a steroid takes hours?', odd:1,
 mean:'<b>问的是三个层级的时间尺度差</b>，而这正是这题的骨架。',
 say:'“Because they act at different levels. Adrenaline works on the second level, the activity of enzymes that already exist. It never enters the cell. It binds a receptor on the surface, raises cyclic AMP, and the kinase modifies enzyme molecules that are already sitting there. Nothing has to be made. A steroid works on the first level, the amount of enzyme. The textbook says steroid hormones enter the cell, bind receptors that are DNA-binding proteins, are carried into the nucleus, and activate certain parts of a gene. That means transcription and then translation, and a protein has to be built before anything changes.”'},

{q:'What would happen if a cell made every enzyme all the time?', odd:1,
 mean:'<b>反过来问同一件事。</b>答案就是书 §10.2 的开场白，加上一笔能量账。',
 say:'“Two things. First, the textbook’s own point. Enzyme reactions would run uselessly in many places, and the effects of some enzyme systems would cancel each other out. If a synthase and a phosphorylase were both active on glycogen, the cell would build and break down the same molecule and turn ATP into heat. Second, the cost. Every enzyme is a protein that costs a transcription and a translation, so making all of them all the time would spend the carbon, nitrogen and energy the textbook says feedback inhibition exists to save.”'},

{q:'What is a rate-limiting step?',
 mean:'书用的是 <i>the rate-limiting reaction</i>（书印的说法），但<b>要跟「committed step」分清楚</b>，他很可能把两个词混着用。',
 say:'“The rate-limiting step is the slowest step, the one whose rate sets the rate of the whole sequence. The committed step is the first step after which the intermediate has no other exit. They are often the same enzyme, and in glycolysis they are. The textbook says the phosphorylation of fructose-6-phosphate is practically irreversible, and that this makes it the rate-limiting reaction of the whole glycolytic process. Glucose-6-phosphate before it is not yet committed, because it can still go into glycogen or into the pentose phosphate pathway.”',
 tail:'<b>「rate-limiting」是书的词，「committed」不是。</b>所以先用书的词，再自己把 committed 定义出来。<span class="pg">p164 p219</span>'}
],

/* ---------------- 追问 ---------------- */
ask:[
{g:'定义类', gn:'他默认你知道这些词。<b>这张卡里有几个词整套卡只有这里用到</b>（flux · compartmentation · isoenzyme · committed step），所以必须自己带定义。', items:[
{r:'高', q:'What is metabolic flux?',
    ez:'“Flux is the rate at which material passes through a pathway. It is usually given as amount per unit time. It is not the same as the rate of one enzyme. A pathway can contain a very fast enzyme. It can still have a low flux. The flux is set at the step that cannot come back.”',
    ezcn:'<b>通量 flux ＝ 物质通过一条通路的速率</b>（<b>通常写成「单位时间多少量」</b>）。⚠ <b>它跟某一个酶的速率不是一回事</b>：<b>一条通路里可以有一个非常快的酶，整条通路的通量却很低</b>——<b>因为通量是在「回不来的那一步」上定下来的。</b>',
 en:'“Flux is the rate at which material passes through a pathway, usually given as amount per unit time. It is not the same as the rate of one enzyme. A pathway can contain a very fast enzyme and still have a low flux, because the flux is set at the step that cannot come back.”',
 cn:'<b>flux 这个词书里没有，整套卡也只有这张用</b>，所以要自己定义。但这个词很值得用，因为它把「速率」和「通量」分开了——他问「调控调的到底是什么」时，答 flux 比答 rate 准确。<br>⚠ <b>不要往「metabolic control analysis / 控制系数」那边走</b>，那是另一门课，这本书完全没有。'},
{r:'高', q:'What is compartmentation?',
    ez:'“The cell is divided into many separate spaces. Those are called compartments. Substrates and products have to be transported across the membranes. Those membranes separate the compartments. The textbook says this transport is usually controlled. That is an important means. It lets synthetic and degradative reactions run separately. It lets them run under control. That holds even when they share some intermediates.”',
    ezcn:'<b>区室化 ＝ 细胞被分成许多彼此分开的空间（区室）</b>，<b>底物和产物必须跨过分隔它们的膜被运输</b>，<b>而书说这种运输通常是受控的</b>。<b>这是一个重要手段：它让合成反应和分解反应分开进行、并且各自受控——哪怕它们共用某些中间物。</b>',
 en:'“The cell is divided into many separate spaces called compartments. Substrates and products have to be transported across the membranes that separate them, and the textbook says this transport is usually controlled. That is an important means of letting synthetic and degradative reactions run separately and under control, even when they share some of their intermediates.”',
 cn:'几乎是书 <span class="pg">p222</span> 的直译。<b>书自己给的理由就是「合成和分解有一部分共同的中间产物」</b>——所以不分开就没法各调各的。<br>实例书在别处：<b>脂肪酸合成在胞质、降解在线粒体</b>（<span class="pg">p220</span>）；<b>柠檬酸循环在基质、呼吸链在内膜</b>（卡 24 / 卡 25）。'},
{r:'中', q:'What is an allosteric enzyme?',
 en:'“An enzyme whose activity is modulated by <b>non-covalent</b> interaction with a specific metabolite. That metabolite binds at a special place in the enzyme molecule called the <b>allosteric site</b>. That place is not the active site. The effect comes about through a change in the conformation of the enzyme. The textbook adds that allosteric enzymes are usually built of subunits, that their own substrates can act as allosteric effectors, and that they have several binding sites for substrate, usually an even number.”',
 cn:'书 <span class="pg">p062</span> 的定义，逐条对得上。<b>「usually an even number」这个细节很像读过书的人才知道的</b>，而且它是 subunit 结构的直接后果。<br><b>变构酶的动力学不服从 Michaelis-Menten</b>：曲线是 <b>S 形</b>不是双曲线（<span class="pg">p063</span>）——这一条留到他追问再给，见「为什么类」那一组。'},
{r:'中', q:'What is feedback inhibition?',
 en:'“It is when the final product of a reaction sequence inhibits one of the enzymes at the beginning of that sequence, and in practice the first one. The textbook calls it negative feedback, says the inhibition is allosteric, and calls such an enzyme the <b>controlling enzyme</b>. It says this is the general mechanism for regulating biosynthetic processes, investigated above all in bacteria, and that it minimises the demand for carbon, nitrogen and energy.”',
 cn:'<b>注意书的措辞是「反应序列开头的某一个酶」</b>（<i>at the reaction steps that are at the beginning of the reaction sequence</i>），不是死死的「第一个」。<b>但它举的两个例子都是第一个酶</b>（Thr → Ile 的脱水酶、ATCase）。<b>所以答「at the beginning, and in the examples the first one」比答死「the first enzyme」稳。</b><span class="pg">p062 p063 p072 p221</span>'},
{r:'中', q:'What is a committed step?',
 en:'“The first step of a pathway after which the intermediate has no other exit, so that once the cell has taken it, the material is committed to that pathway. In glycolysis it is phosphofructokinase and not hexokinase. Glucose-6-phosphate can still go into glycogen or into the pentose phosphate pathway, but fructose-1,6-bisphosphate has nowhere else to go.”',
 cn:'⚠ <b>「committed step」这个词全书 0 命中</b>，所以先给定义再用它，别说成「书上说的」。<b>但它的全部内容书都给了</b>：PFK 那一步实际上不可逆、是限速步（<span class="pg">p164</span>），而 G6P 的两条别的出路书也列了（<span class="pg">p219</span>：糖原、戊糖磷酸途径）。<b>用书的材料搭一个书没有的词，然后说清楚这是你搭的——这是研究者的做法，不是越界。</b>'},
{r:'中', q:'What is an isoenzyme, and how does it regulate anything?',
 en:'“Isoenzymes are enzymatically active proteins that catalyse the same chemical reaction and occur in one organism, but differ in their physical and chemical properties because of genetically determined differences in primary structure. They can differ in their affinity for substrates, coenzymes or inhibitors, and in their thermal or chemical stability. The textbook’s example is lactate dehydrogenase. Five proteins with that activity can be detected in vertebrate tissues, all tetramers, built from two kinds of subunit called H for heart and M for muscle, coded by two different genes on different chromosomes. Enzymes built from H or from M subunits catalyse the same reaction with different kinetic properties, and the textbook says the organism uses this to regulate metabolism.”',
 cn:'<b>§10.2 里「Regulation on the isoenzyme principle」那一节只有一句话，把读者打发去 section 3.7.3</b>——所以内容全在 <span class="pg">p064 p065</span>，要自己去取。<br><b>最后那句是书自己下的结论</b>（<i>the organism uses this for the regulation of metabolism</i>），可以直接引，不用担心越界。<br><b>这一条为什么算调控</b>：同一个反应在不同组织用<b>不同的动力学常数</b>跑，等于给每个组织配了一条量身的曲线——<b>这是唯一一种「靠改变酶本身的身份」来调控的方式</b>，跟前面三个层级都不同。'},
{r:'中', q:'What is a second messenger?',
 en:'“A small molecule made inside the cell in response to a hormone binding outside it, which then carries the signal on to the enzymes. Cyclic AMP is the textbook’s case. The hormone never enters the cell. Adenylate cyclase makes cyclic AMP from ATP, cyclic AMP activates a protein kinase, and the kinase phosphorylates serine and threonine residues of the target proteins. The textbook says the general significance of this mechanism is that an amplifying effect arises through the formation of a second messenger.”',
 cn:'<b>「第二信使」这个词书自己用了</b>（<i>second messenger</i>，书 <span class="pg">p224</span> 印的就是这个词），所以放心说。<br>⚠ <b>放大的倍数不要报数</b>——书没有给。他要数字就给机制：一个受体可以激活多个环化酶分子，一个激酶可以修饰多个底物分子，<b>每一层都是催化，所以是相乘的</b>。英文：“I would not give a number, because each layer is catalytic rather than stoichiometric, so the amplification is a product of the layers rather than a fixed figure.”'},
{r:'中', q:'What is a metabolic pool?',
 en:'“The textbook uses the word for the mobilisable store of a metabolite. It says the concentration can be fairly low, of the order of millimoles per litre, but that the turnover is very fast. What matters is the ability of the organism to keep the level where it is wanted. The metabolites it names as important for regulation are acetyl-CoA, pyruvate, 2-oxoglutarate, the reduced nicotinamide coenzymes NADH and NADPH, and ATP. Acetyl-CoA is its worked example, made by the degradation of sugars, lipids and proteins, and drawn off for the synthesis of fatty acids, lipids, isoprenoids and steroids.”',
 cn:'<b>这五个名字就是代谢图上的「节点」</b>，也是调控真正落手的地方——调一条通路，实际上是在摁住某个节点的水位。<span class="pg">p218 p219</span><br><b>画的顺序（figure 10.1，他可能让你认）</b>：最上一行写 <code>Carbohydrates · Lipids · Proteins</code>；第二行对应写 <code>Pyruvate · Fatty acids · Amino acids</code>；三条箭头往下汇到中间一个 <code>Acetyl-CoA</code>；再从它往下发三条箭头到 <code>Citric acid cycle ＋ Respiratory chain</code>、<code>Lipids</code>、<code>Isoprenoids</code>。<b>上面三进、下面三出，一个漏斗。</b>'},
{r:'中', q:'What does dynamic equilibrium mean here?',
 en:'“The textbook says a living organism is thermodynamically an <b>open system</b>, which covers its need for energy and matter at the expense of its surroundings. Its equilibrium with the surroundings is only apparent, and is better called dynamic, or a stationary state. That is far from the static equilibrium of an isolated system in classical thermodynamics. In the metabolism chapter it uses the same phrase again. Compounds are formed in one place and consumed in another, so the organism is in a dynamic equilibrium.”',
 cn:'<b>这一条是整张卡的地基</b>：静态平衡的系统不需要调控，也不可能被调控。<b>只有一个稳态才有「水位」可言，才有摁得住摁不住的问题。</b><span class="pg">p130 p218</span><br>他要是往下追「什么是 stationary state」，那已经到了物理化学，<b>照停止句说</b>。'}
]},

{g:'为什么类', gn:'<b>这一组是这题真正的分数。</b>手段谁都背得出，理由背不出。每一条都有书的出处，或者明说是推论。', items:[
{r:'高', q:'Why must a controlling enzyme catalyse an irreversible reaction?',
    ez:'“Because only an irreversible step can set a direction. The textbook says a controlling enzyme works this way. It catalyses a practically irreversible reaction. A strongly exergonic one is the example. Now take a step that sits near equilibrium. It is pushed by whichever side has more material. So the cell does not decide it. A step that cannot come back is different. The cell decides it, through the enzyme that catalyses it.”',
    ezcn:'因为<b>只有不可逆的一步才能定方向</b>。书说：<b>控制酶履行职能的方式，就是催化一个实际上不可逆的反应（例如强放能的那种）</b>。<b>一个接近平衡的步骤，是被「哪边东西多」推着走的，细胞说了不算</b>；<b>一个回不来的步骤，细胞才能通过催化它的那个酶来决定。</b>',
 en:'“Because only an irreversible step can set a direction. The textbook says a controlling enzyme can do its job by catalysing a practically irreversible reaction, for example a strongly exergonic one. A step that sits near equilibrium is pushed by whichever side has more material, so the cell does not decide it. A step that cannot come back is decided by the cell, through the enzyme that catalyses it.”',
 cn:'书给的是<b>正面</b>的规矩（<span class="pg">p222</span>），反面那一半是推论。<b>说的时候可以把这个分开交代</b>：“The textbook gives the positive rule. The reason behind it is that a near-equilibrium step is pushed by concentrations rather than decided by the cell.”<br><br><b>=== 公式和推导（背之前先看懂这一段） ===</b><br><b>式子</b>：<code>ΔG°′（偶联后）= ΔG°′（吸能那步）+ ΔG°′（放能那步）</code> —— 书 <span class="pg">p131</span> 明写「一串反应的总 Gibbs 能变等于各步之和」。<br>ΔG°′ 是标准 Gibbs 能变，单位 kJ/mol。<code>&lt; 0</code> 叫 <b>exergonic</b>（放能，自发）；<code>&gt; 0</code> 叫 <b>endergonic</b>（吸能，不自发）。<br><br><b>三步推导</b>（三个数全是书 p131 表里印的）：<br>① <code>glucose + Pᵢ → glucose-6-phosphate + H₂O</code>，<code>ΔG°′ = +13,8</code>：吸能，自己不会走。<br>② <code>ATP + H₂O → ADP + Pᵢ</code>，<code>ΔG°′ = −30,5</code>：放能，自己会走。<br>③ 偶联（hexokinase 那一步）：<code>glucose + ATP → glucose-6-phosphate + ADP</code>，<code>+13,8 − 30,5 = −16,7</code>。<b>书直接把 −16,7 印出来了，还加了一句「这个偶联反应是放能的」。</b><br><br><br><b>=== 一句话理解 ===</b><br><b>那 30,5 里只有 13,8 存进了糖上的磷酸键，剩下 16,7 是故意丢掉的。</b>丢掉的这一份不做功，它买的是「这一步不会往回走」。<br>所以 <b>irreversible 不是一个化学事实，是一笔付过钱的性质</b>——而调控点之所以总坐在这种步骤上，是因为<b>阀门只能装在已经付过钱的地方</b>。<br>顺带把两件事连上了：<b>ΔG 决定方向、动力学（酶）决定速度。</b>调控要同时拿到方向和速度，就只能装在那些 ΔG 已经被拉到很负的步骤上。<br>⚠ 书印的是 <b>ΔG°′（标准态）</b>，细胞里的实际 ΔG 还跟浓度有关；<b>书自己印的说法是「practically irreversible（实际上不可逆）」，那个「实际上」正是书自己留的余地</b>。照书的词说最稳。'},
{r:'高', q:'Why would regulating a near-equilibrium step be pointless?',
    ez:'“Because an enzyme changes only how fast equilibrium is reached. It does not change where that equilibrium lies. The textbook prints the equilibrium compositions itself. At the hexose phosphate isomerase step, fructose six phosphate is thirty per cent. At the aldolase step, eighty nine per cent is hexose. Eleven per cent is the two trioses. At the triose phosphate isomerase step, ninety six per cent is dihydroxyacetone phosphate. Those numbers are properties of the reaction. Doubling the enzyme does not move them.”',
    ezcn:'因为<b>酶只改变「多快到达平衡」，不改变「平衡在哪儿」</b>。<b>书自己把平衡组成印出来了</b>：<b>己糖磷酸异构酶那一步，平衡时果糖-6-磷酸占 30%</b>；<b>醛缩酶那一步，89% 是己糖、11% 是两个丙糖</b>；<b>丙糖磷酸异构酶那一步，96% 是磷酸二羟丙酮</b>。<b>这些数字是反应的性质——把酶加一倍，它们纹丝不动。</b>',
 en:'“Because an enzyme changes how fast an equilibrium is reached and not where that equilibrium lies. The textbook prints the equilibrium compositions itself. At the hexose phosphate isomerase step, fructose-6-phosphate is thirty per cent of the mixture at equilibrium. At the aldolase step, eighty-nine per cent is hexose and eleven per cent is the two trioses. At the triose phosphate isomerase step, ninety-six per cent is dihydroxyacetone phosphate. Those numbers are properties of the reaction. Doubling the enzyme does not move them.”',
 cn:'<b>这一条是「用书的一半打书的另一半」的标准动作。</b>书从来没写「不要控在平衡步」，但它在 <span class="pg">p164</span> 把三个平衡组成印出来了，又在 <span class="pg">p222</span> 说控制酶催化不可逆反应。<b>两处一拼，结论自己出来。</b><br>⚠ <b>别把它说成「改变可逆步的酶量完全没有影响」</b>——那是没有出处的绝对化。<b>能说的只有「平衡的位置不动」。</b><br>他要是接着问「那 triose phosphate isomerase 96% 都是 DHAP，糖酵解还怎么走」——<b>书自己回答了</b>：<i>It is, however, easily converted to glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate, which is consumed by the following reactions</i>。英文：“The textbook answers that itself. The dihydroxyacetone phosphate is easily converted to glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate, and that one is consumed by the following reactions. Pulling the product away keeps the equilibrium step flowing, which is exactly why such a step cannot control anything.”<b>这一句非常漂亮，因为它同时证明了这条规矩。</b>'},
{r:'高', q:'Why are catabolic and anabolic routes different rather than the same route run backwards?',
    ez:'“The textbook gives the reason directly. It is the fundamental difference in their energy demands. Catabolic processes produce A T P. Anabolic reactions require energy. They consume A T P as they run. A single reversible route could not do both. The textbook then draws the regulatory conclusion itself. The routes are separate. So they can be regulated independently. It adds one more point. They are often in different parts of the eukaryotic cell. So both can run at the same time and still independently.”',
    ezcn:'<b>书直接给了理由：两者在能量需求上有根本差别。</b><b>分解代谢产生 ATP；合成代谢需要能量，一边跑一边消耗 ATP</b>——<b>一条可逆的路线没法同时干这两件事</b>。<b>书接着自己推出调控上的结论：正因为路线是分开的，它们才能被独立调控</b>。<b>它还补了一句：在真核细胞里它们常常位于不同的部位，所以两者可以同时进行、而且仍然各自独立。</b>',
 en:'“The textbook gives the reason directly. It is the fundamental difference in their energy demands. Catabolic processes produce ATP, while anabolic reactions require energy and consume ATP as they run. A single reversible route could not do both. And the textbook then draws the regulatory conclusion itself. Because the routes are separate, they can be regulated independently. It adds that they are often localised in different parts of the eukaryotic cell, so both can run at the same time and still independently.”',
 cn:'<b>这一条是整张卡的前提</b>，不是一个例子。没有它，前面三个层级都无处落脚：同一条路的正反方向共用一套酶，你就不可能只关掉一个方向。<span class="pg">p218 p134</span><br><b>三个现成实例</b>：① 脂肪酸合成用 NADPH 在胞质、降解产 NADH 在线粒体（<span class="pg">p220</span>）；② 糖异生绕道 pyruvate → oxaloacetate → PEP，两次己糖磷酸化不可逆、合成时换酶（<span class="pg">p219</span>）；③ 戊糖磷酸途径的 NADPH 不进呼吸链（<span class="pg">p219</span>）。<br>⚠ <b>书 p219 把糖异生里替换的酶只写成 hydrolases（水解酶）</b>，跟通用写法（葡萄糖-6-磷酸酶、果糖-1,6-二磷酸酶，确实是磷酸酶＝水解酶的一类）方向一致，<b>但书没有给酶名</b>。他要名字时说「the textbook only says the two phosphorylations must be catalysed by different enzymes, hydrolases」，<b>不要替书补名字</b>。'},
{r:'高', q:'Why does the cell keep NADH and NADPH as two separate pools?',
    ez:'“So that oxidation and reduction can run at the same time. They run in the same cell without cancelling out. The textbook makes the split twice. The pentose phosphate pathway makes N A D P H. The textbook says it is not oxidised in the respiratory chain. It is used for synthetic reactions. And in fatty acid metabolism it says the same thing. The hydrogen donor for synthesis is N A D P H. Oxidation gives N A D H. So two almost identical coenzymes are kept in two pools. That lets the cell run both directions independently. It is compartmentation done in chemistry rather than in space.”',
    ezcn:'<b>为了让氧化和还原能在同一个细胞里同时进行而不互相抵消。</b>书把这个分工讲了两次：<b>戊糖磷酸途径产生的 NADPH 不进呼吸链被氧化，而是用于合成反应</b>；<b>脂肪酸代谢里也一样——合成的供氢体是 NADPH，氧化给出的是 NADH</b>。<b>两个化学上几乎一样的辅酶，被分成两个池子，于是细胞能独立地跑两个方向。</b><b>这是用化学做出来的区室化，不是用空间。</b>',
 en:'“So that oxidation and reduction can run at the same time in the same cell without cancelling each other out. The textbook makes the split twice. It says the NADPH formed in the pentose phosphate pathway is not oxidised in the respiratory chain but is used for synthetic reactions. And in fatty acid metabolism it says the hydrogen donor for synthesis is NADPH, while oxidation gives NADH. Two chemically almost identical coenzymes, kept in two pools, let the cell run the two directions independently. It is compartmentation carried out in chemistry rather than in space.”',
 cn:'<b>最后那一句是这条的落点</b>，而且它是个很漂亮的说法：<b>区室化不一定靠膜，也可以靠「换一个几乎一样的分子」。</b><span class="pg">p219 p220</span><br>他要是追「两者化学上差在哪」——<b>差一个 2′-磷酸</b>：“NADP differs from NAD by a phosphate on the 2-prime hydroxyl of the adenosine ribose, the second carbon of that sugar ring. It changes almost nothing chemically and everything about which enzymes will accept it.”<br>⚠ <b>这本书有没有印那个 2′-磷酸，我没有逐页核过</b>；说的时候用「generally」带一句，别说「书上说」。'},
{r:'高', q:'How does a cell know how much energy it has?',
    ez:'“Through the ratio of the adenine nucleotides. The textbook puts it concretely. It says phosphofructokinase is allosterically controlled. It is controlled by the A T P and A D P system. So the same molecule the pathway produces also slows the pathway down. A T P is both the product and the inhibitor. A D P is the signal that there is room to make more. That is feedback inhibition. It acts on the cell’s energy state. It does not act on one end product.”',
    ezcn:'<b>靠腺苷酸之间的比例。</b>书说得很具体：<b>磷酸果糖激酶受 ATP/ADP 系统的别构控制</b>。<b>于是这条通路产生的那个分子，同时也是让这条通路慢下来的信号</b>：<b>ATP 既是产物也是抑制剂，ADP 则是「还有余地，可以多做」的信号</b>。<b>这是作用在细胞能量状态上的反馈抑制，而不是作用在某一个终产物上。</b>',
 en:'“Through the ratio of the adenine nucleotides. The textbook puts it concretely. It says the activity of phosphofructokinase is allosterically controlled by the <b>ATP and ADP system</b>. So the same molecule that the pathway produces is also the signal that slows the pathway down. ATP is both the product and the inhibitor, and ADP is the signal that there is room to make more. That is feedback inhibition acting on the cell’s energy state rather than on one end product.”',
 cn:'<b>这是「能量荷」这个概念的书内版本，而且照书说比报术语稳</b>——<b>「energy charge」全书 0 命中——英文写法和书本身语言里的写法都查过。</b><span class="pg">p164</span><br>他要是自己说出 energy charge 这个词，你要接得住，但要先声明出处：“That term is not in this textbook, but the quantity is standard.”<br><br><b>=== 公式和推导（背之前先看懂这一段） ===</b><br><b>式子</b>：<code>EC = ([ATP] + ½[ADP]) / ([ATP] + [ADP] + [AMP])</code><br>方括号是浓度 · 分母是三种腺苷酸的<b>总量</b>（adenylate pool，总量基本不变）· EC 是一个 0 到 1 之间的纯数。<br><br><b>四步推导</b>（那个 ½ 不是拟合出来的系数，是数出来的）：<br>① 数<b>可转移的酸酐磷酸键</b>：ATP 有 <b>2</b> 个，ADP 有 <b>1</b> 个，AMP 有 <b>0</b> 个。<br>② <b>满电</b>时三种全是 ATP，总键数 <code>= 2 × 总量</code>。<br>③ <b>实际</b>键数 <code>= 2[ATP] + 1[ADP]</code>。<br>④ 充电比例 <code>= (2[ATP] + [ADP]) / (2 × 总量) = ([ATP] + ½[ADP]) / 总量</code>。<b>½ 就是「ADP 只带半份电」。</b><br><br><br><b>=== 一句话理解 ===</b><br>这是一块电池的<b>电量表</b>：全是 ATP 时读 <b>1</b>，全是 AMP 时读 <b>0</b>。<br>细胞不是去测「我有多少 ATP」，而是测<b>「充电到了几成」</b>——因为腺苷酸总量几乎不变，所以比例才是信息，绝对量不是。<b>这正好回应 pool 那一格：重要的是水位，不是池子大小。</b><br>⚠ <b>「细胞通常维持在 0,8 到 0,95 之间」是通用知识，这本书里没有，我也没有核过原始出处</b>——<b>考场上不要报这个区间</b>。要说就说判据：“The quantity is a ratio between zero and one, and the cell holds it in a narrow band. I would not quote a figure, because it is not in this textbook.”<b>报一个撑不住的数是这场考试里最危险的动作。</b>'},
{r:'高', q:'Why a cascade rather than one switch?',
    ez:'“Because each layer is catalytic. So the layers multiply. The textbook makes the point twice. In the covalent modification section it says the activation is itself catalysed. A specific enzyme catalyses it. So when it is needed, the signal can be considerably amplified. The result is a large supply of energy. In the hormone section it says something similar. The significance of the cyclic A M P mechanism lies in the amplifying effect. That effect arises through a second messenger. A cascade also gives more than one place to intervene. So the same signal can be tuned differently in different tissues.”',
    ezcn:'因为<b>每一层都是催化性的，所以层与层之间是相乘的</b>。书说了两次：<b>在共价修饰那一节——把无活性形式转成活性形式这件事本身由一个专一的酶催化，所以需要的时候信号可以被大幅放大，结果是一大笔能量供应</b>；<b>在激素那一节——cAMP 机制的普遍意义在于通过第二信使产生的放大效应</b>。<b>另外，级联还提供了不止一个可以介入的位置，所以同一个信号能在不同组织里被调成不同的样子。</b>',
 en:'“Because each layer is catalytic, so the layers multiply. The textbook makes the point twice. In the covalent modification section it says the conversion of the inactive form into the active one is catalysed by a specific enzyme. So when it is needed, the signal can be considerably amplified, and the result is a large supply of energy. In the hormone section it says the general significance of the cyclic AMP mechanism lies in the amplifying effect that arises through the formation of a second messenger. And a cascade gives more than one place to intervene, so the same signal can be tuned differently in different tissues.”',
 cn:'<b>放大是级联唯一的理由，而书两处都写了，所以可以放心说。</b><span class="pg">p063 p224</span><br>⚠ <b>倍数不要报数，书没给。</b>他追就给机制：每一层都是催化而不是化学计量，所以放大是各层<b>相乘</b>而不是相加。<br><b>书还给了第二个好处</b>（<span class="pg">p224</span>）：<b>同一个基本原理，不同细胞按各自的受体装备和对 cAMP 敏感的酶系统给出不同的回答</b>。这一条解释了为什么肾上腺素在肝、在肌肉、在心脏做的事不一样——<b>信号是一样的，读信号的人不一样。</b>'},
{r:'中', q:'Is the Michaelis-Menten equation enough to describe a regulatory enzyme?',
 en:'“No, and the textbook says so. For the kinetics of allosteric enzymes the Michaelis-Menten equation does not hold, and the dependence of rate on substrate concentration is not hyperbolic but <b>sigmoid</b>. The reason it gives is cooperativity. Binding the first substrate molecule raises the affinity of the enzyme for the next ones. So over a certain range of concentration, a very small change in substrate causes a much larger change in rate than a non-regulatory enzyme would show. That is called positive cooperativity. Negative cooperativity is the opposite, where the first bound substrate lowers the affinity for the next ones and the rate rises only very slowly.”',
 cn:'<b>这一条把卡 02 接进了调控题，而且是书自己接的。</b><span class="pg">p063</span><br><b>S 形曲线的意义一句话</b>：双曲线在任何浓度上都是「加一点底物、快一点点」，<b>S 形有一段是「加一点底物、快很多」——那一段就是可调的工作区</b>。<b>调控酶要的不是快，是「陡」。</b><br>书同页还有 <b>figure 3.7</b>，画的是变构酶的两条曲线：<b>1 ＝ 抑制（曲线右移、更平），2 ＝ 活化（曲线左移、更陡）</b>，横轴 [A]，纵轴 v，并标出 <code>S₀,₅</code>。<b>他摆这张图问「这是什么」，答「an allosteric enzyme, sigmoid because of cooperativity, and the two curves are inhibition and activation」。</b>'}
]},

{g:'机制细节', gn:'第二层级那三条，各自往下一层。<b>他从这题走下去，八成走这里。</b>', items:[
{r:'高', q:'What exactly is covalent modification?',
    ez:'“Some enzymes can pass from one form into another. They go from a less active or inactive form into an active one. Or the other way round. A chemical modification does it. That modification is itself catalysed by an enzyme. So a regulatory enzyme can be the substrate of another enzyme. The most common case is phosphorylation. The textbook’s example is glycogen phosphorylase. Form a is active. Form b is almost inactive. They interconvert by reversible phosphorylation. The phosphate goes on a serine hydroxyl. That serine is in the active centre of each subunit. A specific phosphatase takes it off again. It does that when the need for energy has passed.”',
    ezcn:'<b>共价修饰 ＝ 有些酶能通过一个化学修饰，从活性较低或完全无活性的形式转成活性形式（或者反过来）</b>，<b>而那个修饰本身由一个酶催化</b>——<b>所以一个调控酶可以是另一个酶的底物</b>。<b>最常见的是磷酸化</b>。书的例子是<b>糖原磷酸化酶</b>：<b>a 型有活性，b 型几乎没有，靠可逆磷酸化互换</b>（<b>磷酸加在每个亚基活性中心里一个丝氨酸的羟基上</b>），<b>能量需求过去之后，一个专一的磷酸酶把它取下来</b>。',
 en:'“Some enzymes can pass from a less active or completely inactive form into an active form, or the other way round, by a chemical modification. That modification is itself catalysed by an enzyme, so a regulatory enzyme can be the substrate of another enzyme. The most common case is <b>phosphorylation</b>. The textbook’s example is glycogen phosphorylase. Form a is active and form b is almost inactive, and they interconvert by reversible phosphorylation of the hydroxyl of a serine in the active centre of each subunit. A specific phosphatase takes the phosphate off again when the need for energy has passed.”',
 cn:'书 <span class="pg">p063 p222</span>。<b>「一个调节酶可以是另一个酶的底物」这一句是这条的关键</b>——它说明共价修饰天然是<b>级联</b>的，而变构不是。<br><b>另一类是不可逆的</b>：无活性的 <b>proenzyme / zymogen</b> 经<b>部分蛋白水解</b>变成活性酶（trypsinogen → trypsin）。书 <span class="pg">p064</span> 明说 <i>this type of regulation is this time irreversible</i>，并列了 trypsin、chymotrypsin、pepsin 和凝血酶系。<b>整套在卡 08。</b>'},
{r:'高', q:'Allosteric or covalent — how do you choose which one to talk about?',
    ez:'“They answer different questions. Allosteric control is non covalent and instant. The effector binds. The conformation changes. The effector leaves. And nothing is spent. So it reports a concentration. That makes it right for feedback from inside the pathway. Covalent modification is different. It needs another enzyme. It costs a phosphate from A T P. And it persists until a phosphatase removes it. It can amplify, because each layer is catalytic. So it is right for a signal from outside the cell. A hormone is that kind of signal. The textbook uses allosteric control for feedback inhibition. It uses covalent modification for the adrenaline cascade.”',
    ezcn:'<b>它们回答的是不同的问题。</b><b>别构控制是非共价的、瞬时的</b>：<b>效应物结合 → 构象改变 → 效应物离开，什么也没花掉</b>——<b>它报告的是一个浓度，所以适合通路内部的反馈</b>。<b>共价修饰要另一个酶、要花一个来自 ATP 的磷酸，而且会一直保持到磷酸酶把它取下来</b>；<b>它能放大，因为每一层都是催化性的</b>——<b>所以它适合来自细胞外的信号，比如激素</b>。<b>书正是这么分的：反馈抑制用别构，肾上腺素和胰高血糖素的级联用共价修饰。</b>',
 en:'“They answer different questions. Allosteric control is non-covalent and instant. The effector binds, the conformation changes, the effector leaves, and nothing is spent. It reports a concentration, so it is the right mechanism for feedback from inside the pathway. Covalent modification needs another enzyme and costs a phosphate from ATP, and it persists until a phosphatase removes it. It can amplify, because each layer is catalytic. So it is the right mechanism for a signal that comes from outside the cell, like a hormone. The textbook uses allosteric control for feedback inhibition and covalent modification for the adrenaline and glucagon cascade, and that is exactly the division.”',
 cn:'<b>这个对照表是自己整理的，但每一格都有书的出处</b>：变构非共价可逆 <span class="pg">p062</span>、共价修饰由另一个酶催化且可放大 <span class="pg">p063</span>、反馈抑制是变构的 <span class="pg">p063 p221</span>、肾上腺素走 cAMP 级联 <span class="pg">p174 p223</span>。<br><b>「内部的信号用变构，外部的信号用共价修饰」这一句是全卡最好用的一个概括</b>——他很可能顺着问「为什么」，你就把上面那三条理由报出来。'},
{r:'中', q:'How is a hormone signal switched off?',
 en:'“Three ways, and the textbook gives two of them. Cyclic AMP is quickly broken down in the cell to AMP by a <b>phosphodiesterase</b>, an enzyme that hydrolyses the cyclic ring open. The half-life of cyclic AMP is short. A covalent modification is removed by a specific phosphatase, and the textbook names the specific protein phosphatases that take phosphate off the phosphorylated enzymes of the glycogen cascade. The third is the gland itself. The textbook shows a three-step hierarchy from the hypothalamic neurosecretion through the glandotropic hormone of the pituitary to cortisol from the adrenal cortex, and cortisol inhibits the steps above it.”',
 cn:'<b>第三条最值钱</b>，因为它把反馈抑制从一条通路放大到了整个机体：<b>同一个原则，两个尺度。</b>书 <span class="pg">p222</span> 的图上给那条回头的箭头明写了 <i>inhibition</i> 这个词。<br><b>「能打开的必须能关掉」是这一格的收口</b>：“A signalling system needs a way to be switched off, and not only a way to be switched on.”'},
{r:'中', q:'What does the adenylate cyclase cascade actually do, step by step?',
 en:'“The hormone binds a receptor on the cell surface and does not enter the cell. Adenylate cyclase then makes <b>cyclic AMP</b> from ATP, and the cyclic form is a ring between the phosphate and the three-prime hydroxyl of the same ribose. Cyclic AMP activates a <b>protein kinase</b>. The kinase phosphorylates serine and threonine residues of the target proteins, and that is what changes their activity. Adrenaline and glucagon are the textbook’s examples. A phosphodiesterase then hydrolyses cyclic AMP back to AMP, so the signal decays.”',
 cn:'书 <span class="pg">p223 p224</span> 的图把 <b>ATP → cyclic AMP → AMP</b> 三个结构都画出来了，两个酶名标在箭头上（<b>adenylate cyclase</b> 和 <b>phosphodiesterase</b>）。<br><b>画的顺序（他可能让你画这条线，不是画结构式）</b>：横着写 <code>hormone → receptor（膜上）→ adenylate cyclase → cAMP → protein kinase → 目标蛋白的 Ser/Thr 磷酸化</code>，再从 cAMP 往下拉一条短箭头写 <code>phosphodiesterase → AMP</code>。<b>六个格子，十秒画完，而且每个箭头上都有一个书里的名字。</b><br>⚠ <b>那个三元环的连接方式（3′ 和 5′）书的图上有，但 OCR 读不出来</b>；说 “a ring closed onto the ribose” 就够，被逼问再说 <b>3′,5′-cyclic AMP</b>。'}
]},

{g:'硬事实', gn:'不背，眼熟即可。<b>specific 题的弹药</b>——他问「书里点名的调控酶有哪些」时你要报得出。', items:[
{r:'中', q:'书 §10.2 列的调控机制，全表',
 en:'“The textbook lists them under its own headings. Regulation through Michaelis kinetics, that is by substrate concentration. Regulation by changing the level of the enzyme, by induction or repression. Inhibition of enzyme reactions, including inhibition by the product. Allosteric control, including negative feedback. The controlling reaction, an enzyme catalysing a practically irreversible step. Regulation by limiting metabolites. Regulation by enzyme modification, phosphorylation and proteolytic activation. Regulation on the isoenzyme principle. And compartmentation.”',
 cn:'<b>九条，就是 §10.2 的九个小标题</b>（<span class="pg">p221 p222</span>）。<b>照原顺序报一遍，很难不加分。</b><br>⚠ <b>但不要只报这个清单就完事</b>——那正好是这题最容易掉进去的坑（目录 vs 理解）。<b>先给三个层级的框架，再说「书自己是按九条列的，我把它们归成三层」</b>，这样既显得读过书，又显得理解了。英文：“The textbook lists nine mechanisms. I have grouped them into three levels, because the nine are mechanisms and the three are principles.”'},
{r:'中', q:'书里点名的调控酶和调控点',
 en:'“<b>Phosphofructokinase</b>, rate-limiting in glycolysis and allosterically controlled by the ATP and ADP system. <b>Glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate dehydrogenase</b>, which the textbook calls an allosteric enzyme whose most important activating effector is NAD-plus. <b>Aspartate transcarbamoylase</b>, allosteric, inhibited by CTP, the end product of pyrimidine nucleotide synthesis. The <b>threonine dehydratase</b> that begins isoleucine synthesis, inhibited by isoleucine. <b>Glycogen phosphorylase</b> and <b>glycogen synthase</b>, reciprocally switched by one protein kinase. <b>HMG-CoA reductase</b>, which the textbook calls the key regulatory enzyme of terpenoid biosynthesis and links to cholesterol and atherosclerosis. And <b>delta-aminolevulinate synthase</b>, which it calls the key point of regulation of porphyrin synthesis.”',
 cn:'<b>七个，全部有页码</b>：PFK <span class="pg">p164 p222</span> · GAPDH <span class="pg">p165</span> · ATCase <span class="pg">p072</span> · Thr 脱水酶 <span class="pg">p063</span> · 糖原两酶 <span class="pg">p174 p175</span> · HMG-CoA 还原酶 <span class="pg">p193</span> · ALA 合酶 <span class="pg">p146</span>。<br><b>再加一个在卡 24 上的</b>：柠檬酸循环的 <b>citrate synthase</b>，被 ATP 强烈抑制。<br><b>这一串的用法</b>：他问「举个例子」时报一个就够；他问「书里还有别的吗」时你能连报七个——<b>那一刻他会停止挖这道题。</b>'},
{r:'中', q:'激素表里最值得记的几行',
 en:'“The textbook groups hormones into four chemical classes. Steroid hormones, hormones derived from amino acids, peptide and protein hormones, and eikosanoids. Eikosanoids contain twenty carbon atoms, and eikosi is Greek for twenty. From its table, the ones that matter for metabolic regulation are these. <b>Insulin</b> from the pancreas lowers blood sugar. <b>Glucagon</b> from the pancreas raises it. <b>Adrenaline</b> from the adrenal medulla is the response to short-term stress, raising pulse, raising blood glucose and breaking down glycogen. <b>Cortisol</b> from the adrenal cortex is the response to stress. And <b>thyroxine</b> from the thyroid raises the basal metabolic rate.”',
 cn:'书 <b>table 10.1</b>（<span class="pg">p225 p226</span>），OCR 出来很碎，上面这五行是能读清的。<b>「eikosi 是希腊文的二十」这个词源书自己写了</b>，说出来很像读过书的人。<br>⚠ <b>整张表不要背</b>——它是内分泌学，不是这道题。<b>记住这五个，因为它们全都直接作用在代谢上</b>，正好接回这张卡：胰岛素和胰高血糖素是一对<b>互控</b>的激素，肾上腺素走的是 cAMP 级联，皮质醇是三级回路的终点。'},
{r:'中', q:'书印的那些数（只有这几个，别自己加）',
 en:'“The textbook calculates that the human body makes about seventy kilograms of ATP in twenty-four hours, while the concentration of such a metabolite can be of the order of millimoles per litre. For the free energies it prints the phosphorylation of glucose at plus thirteen point eight kilojoules per mole, the hydrolysis of ATP at minus thirty point five, and the coupled reaction at minus sixteen point seven. And it prints three equilibria in glycolysis. Thirty per cent fructose-6-phosphate at the isomerase step. Eighty-nine per cent hexose against eleven per cent trioses at the aldolase step. And ninety-six per cent dihydroxyacetone phosphate at the triose phosphate isomerase step.”',
 cn:'<b>这一格的用途是划一条线：线内的数可以报，线外的数一个都不要编。</b><span class="pg">p131 p164 p219</span><br><b>明确不在书里、因此不要报的数</b>：能量荷的数值区间 · cAMP 级联的放大倍数 · 体内 ATP 的总量 · 任何一个酶的 Km 或 Ki 的具体值 · 「一条通路只有一个控制点」这种计数。<b>他追问时给判据 ＋ 给反例，不要给数</b>（见 LOGIC §11）。<br>英文（他要一个书里没有的数时用）：“That figure is not in this textbook and I would rather not quote one. What I can give you is the criterion.”'}
]}
]
});
